WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.100
Welcome to Jamovi.

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:05.800
Jamovi is an application
for analyzing data and helping

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:09.033
you make sense of the information
around you.

00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:14.133
It's also a compelling alternative
to expensive proprietary programs

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:17.366
like SPSS and SAS and Stata.

00:00:17.766 --> 00:00:22.000
Instead,
Jamovi is based on the open source

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:26.400
programing language R,
which is designed for working with data.

00:00:26.933 --> 00:00:29.666
And what that means is that JR
movie is free.

00:00:30.333 --> 00:00:34.166
It's open
and it's extraordinarily friendly.

00:00:34.566 --> 00:00:38.300
In fact, once you start using a movie,
you'll simply be embarrassed

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:40.500
that you didn't start using it sooner.

00:00:41.333 --> 00:00:43.866
Now I want to mention
one thing about this course.

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:48.166
This is a tools course
and teaching you how to use software.

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:51.633
It works best
if you already know a little about data,

00:00:51.633 --> 00:00:54.866
because I'm not going to be
discussing the principles at great length.

00:00:55.466 --> 00:00:56.433
On the other hand, it's

00:00:56.433 --> 00:01:00.133
easier to learn data
when you have a good tool like a movie.

00:01:00.433 --> 00:01:03.633
So it really, either way, works fine.

00:01:04.066 --> 00:01:07.233
Just get started and see what you can do.

00:01:07.733 --> 00:01:11.166
Go to the movie
Dot org and download the program

00:01:11.666 --> 00:01:17.100
and then go to DataLab Dot
CC slash tools, slash GMV to download

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:19.266
all the course files
so you can follow along

00:01:19.266 --> 00:01:22.200
with what I'm doing
and start working in your own data too.

00:01:23.066 --> 00:01:24.900
And with that, let's go.

00:01:26.033 --> 00:01:26.966
The first thing you need

00:01:26.966 --> 00:01:31.066
to do to work with your movie
is to get it installed on your computer.

00:01:31.266 --> 00:01:34.433
It's a desktop application,
so you'll need to have either Windows,

00:01:34.433 --> 00:01:37.300
Mac or Linux
and you install it and run it locally.

00:01:37.766 --> 00:01:41.800
Go to Jam Movie Dawg,
and then simply click on this link.

00:01:42.033 --> 00:01:46.466
Download Now the beautiful thing is
it's free and it's open source.

00:01:47.233 --> 00:01:49.333
Click the in system that you need.

00:01:49.366 --> 00:01:51.700
Windows Mac OS or Linux.

00:01:52.133 --> 00:01:55.500
Download the file double click on it
and you'll be ready

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:58.700
to start working into movie
in just a few seconds.

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:02.900
When you open your movie,
this is the window that you're going to

00:02:02.900 --> 00:02:06.900
see. Now, if you're used to
some other applications like SPSS or SAS,

00:02:07.233 --> 00:02:10.966
what's a little different here
is that it's a single window, actually,

00:02:10.966 --> 00:02:15.100
that makes it really easy to organize
and to navigate through it.

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:17.866
But I want to show you
a few different parts of what's going on

00:02:18.766 --> 00:02:21.766
now over here on the left,
of course, where we have rows and columns

00:02:21.766 --> 00:02:23.966
arranged, integrated.
This is where your data goes.

00:02:23.966 --> 00:02:25.500
It's your data window.

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:28.600
Right now, we have three
variables, just called A, B and C.

00:02:28.866 --> 00:02:30.933
You're, of course, going to replace those
with something else.

00:02:30.933 --> 00:02:32.366
And you put the data in.

00:02:32.366 --> 00:02:36.366
So it's a one column
per variable, one row per case

00:02:37.633 --> 00:02:40.733
over here on the right is the output
window.

00:02:40.733 --> 00:02:42.933
It's where we'll see the results
of our analyzes.

00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:46.066
Up at the top, we have two tabs here.

00:02:46.066 --> 00:02:47.033
The first one that's open

00:02:47.033 --> 00:02:50.800
by default is analyzes,
which gives you options for exploration.

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:55.100
If I click on that and come
to descriptive, you'll see, for instance,

00:02:55.100 --> 00:02:59.566
it opens up the menu that allows you
to choose what you want to do

00:02:59.566 --> 00:03:02.733
and you get a blank version

00:03:02.733 --> 00:03:05.733
of the results over here on the right

00:03:05.733 --> 00:03:08.700
by the way,
you'll notice that the menus into movie

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:11.866
are designed to resemble those of SPSS.

00:03:12.366 --> 00:03:14.433
It's not based on SPSS.

00:03:14.433 --> 00:03:19.133
Your movie actually runs on R,
but it's designed to make it easy

00:03:19.133 --> 00:03:24.100
for people who are used to SPSS
to really migrate over to G movie.

00:03:24.666 --> 00:03:27.300
You can close the windows
by clicking on this arrow,

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:30.800
hide the options
and we have versions of T tests,

00:03:31.633 --> 00:03:36.400
the analysis of variance
or ANOVA or ANOVA regression

00:03:37.533 --> 00:03:39.466
frequencies

00:03:39.666 --> 00:03:41.166
and then factor.

00:03:41.166 --> 00:03:45.100
And obviously I'll go over each of these
procedures in separate videos.

00:03:46.100 --> 00:03:48.133
You also have a data tab right here

00:03:48.900 --> 00:03:53.533
that allows you to paste in your
information to do a setup of the variables

00:03:53.533 --> 00:03:57.666
where you define what's in them,
the type it calls them continuous,

00:03:57.666 --> 00:04:03.866
although I would call that measured or
scaled or quantitative or null or nominal.

00:04:03.866 --> 00:04:04.766
And then you can put down

00:04:04.766 --> 00:04:08.866
integer decimal or text,
you can give levels, you can give names,

00:04:09.433 --> 00:04:13.800
you can compute new variables,
you can add elite variables in columns,

00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:18.100
you can filter your cases and add
or delete rows for cases.

00:04:18.766 --> 00:04:21.866
I can hide this right here

00:04:22.100 --> 00:04:23.400
at the far left,

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:28.466
the three horizontal two lines
give you the menu to new open save

00:04:28.466 --> 00:04:33.433
and so on, as well as recent data
you have at the bottom.

00:04:33.433 --> 00:04:37.033
And then one more that's really
important is way over here at the right

00:04:38.166 --> 00:04:38.566
click on

00:04:38.566 --> 00:04:42.300
the three dots
and you get a number of options zooming

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:46.500
and the number format one interesting
one here is the plot theme.

00:04:46.500 --> 00:04:49.066
So when it makes the graphs,

00:04:49.066 --> 00:04:52.400
if you really want it to look like SPSS,
you can get the base charts.

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:53.600
I don't like them.

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:56.033
I actually prefer minimal.

00:04:56.033 --> 00:04:58.400
Hadlee is a reference to Hadley Wickham.

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:01.700
The person who developed Gigi,
applied to a very common graphics program

00:05:01.700 --> 00:05:04.966
for our users
and then minimal is also really nice.

00:05:04.966 --> 00:05:08.366
I'll keep it at default for now
and then syntax mode.

00:05:08.366 --> 00:05:11.833
If you click on this, then you're actually
going to be able to see the

00:05:11.866 --> 00:05:16.233
R language syntax
using the gem V package that allows you

00:05:16.233 --> 00:05:20.633
to duplicate any of these features,
any of these analyzes in R.

00:05:20.633 --> 00:05:24.800
That's an enormous benefit,
especially if you're familiar with SPSS,

00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:29.466
but you're trying to learn are.

00:05:29.466 --> 00:05:33.633
And so that's a general orientation
of what's going on in your movie

00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:35.100
in the Future videos.

00:05:35.100 --> 00:05:37.233
I'll show you
how each of these options work.

00:05:37.233 --> 00:05:41.100
Each of the analyzes function
and how to read some of the output that

00:05:41.100 --> 00:05:42.100
you get from your movie.

00:05:43.166 --> 00:05:43.800
The fastest

00:05:43.800 --> 00:05:48.533
way to get started with your movie
is to explore its example data sets.

00:05:48.966 --> 00:05:51.766
To do that, come up to the little menu,
the three

00:05:51.766 --> 00:05:54.566
horizontal lines
here, and come down to open.

00:05:55.666 --> 00:05:58.366
Now in this PC means the ones that are
installed on your computer.

00:05:58.366 --> 00:06:01.666
But examples
there are four sample data sets.

00:06:01.666 --> 00:06:04.000
The first one consists of big five data.

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:06.566
This is big five personality
characteristics.

00:06:07.166 --> 00:06:09.166
I'll click on that and it opens up.

00:06:09.166 --> 00:06:14.233
And what it has is five variables
on the personality characteristics

00:06:14.233 --> 00:06:18.266
neuroticism, extroversion, openness
to experience,

00:06:18.266 --> 00:06:22.100
agreeableness and conscientiousness,
and they're rated on 1 to 5 scales.

00:06:22.666 --> 00:06:25.766
And this is a great data
set for doing correlations,

00:06:25.766 --> 00:06:28.733
for doing regression,
and for doing data exploration.

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:32.233
Another good dataset,

00:06:32.233 --> 00:06:35.766
again to open an examples
is to boost growth.

00:06:35.833 --> 00:06:37.800
Now this kind of a funny data set

00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:41.000
because it has to do
with the length of teeth in guinea pigs.

00:06:41.566 --> 00:06:45.300
But what it has is a single outcome
variable that's called length,

00:06:45.333 --> 00:06:48.666
that's for length,
and then sub is for the supplement.

00:06:48.666 --> 00:06:51.533
This is an experiment
where they are giving the guinea pigs

00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:54.933
one of two different supplements
to aid in their tooth growth.

00:06:55.266 --> 00:06:57.866
It's either V.S., which is for a vitamin C

00:06:58.333 --> 00:07:00.400
or it's OJ for orange juice.

00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:04.600
And then on top of that,
they manipulate the dosage

00:07:04.600 --> 00:07:07.266
either 500, 1000 or 2000.

00:07:07.800 --> 00:07:10.300
And this allows you
to look at the combined effect of these

00:07:10.300 --> 00:07:13.933
two factors
on the growth of teeth in guinea pigs.

00:07:14.133 --> 00:07:18.666
It's a good way to get started
with the analysis of variance.

00:07:18.666 --> 00:07:19.400
After that,

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:22.733
there is bugs.

00:07:22.733 --> 00:07:25.500
This is a peculiar data set
because it has to do

00:07:25.500 --> 00:07:28.566
with how people react to insects.

00:07:28.566 --> 00:07:33.300
We have a subject number gender male,
only female and region some education.

00:07:33.300 --> 00:07:35.666
And then these four mysterious variables

00:07:36.833 --> 00:07:39.300
are the level of disgusting ness

00:07:39.300 --> 00:07:41.933
and the level of frightening ness,

00:07:42.366 --> 00:07:44.966
either low or high on the both of them.

00:07:45.300 --> 00:07:48.966
And what this is, is a good example
of the analysis of variance

00:07:48.966 --> 00:07:53.333
for a repeated measures or within
subject variation, because everybody

00:07:53.333 --> 00:07:57.900
in this experiment gave responses
to all four possible categories.

00:07:57.900 --> 00:08:00.366
And the final data set

00:08:00.366 --> 00:08:04.166
under again
examples is the classic Anderson's

00:08:04.200 --> 00:08:07.266
Iris data, also known as Fisher's
Iris Data.

00:08:07.566 --> 00:08:11.866
And what this is, is the measurement
of the Siebel length and Siebel

00:08:11.866 --> 00:08:16.200
width and pedal length and pedal
width of three species of irises.

00:08:16.433 --> 00:08:19.233
So you have four quantitative
or continuous variables

00:08:19.566 --> 00:08:21.900
and one nominal or categorical variable.

00:08:22.733 --> 00:08:26.000
This is a classic data set
on how quantitative

00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:29.333
measurements can be used to place
observations into categories.

00:08:29.333 --> 00:08:33.733
It's also a great way to practice
breaking down explorations by groups

00:08:33.733 --> 00:08:37.200
because these species of iris differ
pretty substantially

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:40.866
in some of these measurements,
and so use any of these four data

00:08:40.866 --> 00:08:44.400
sets as a way to start exploring
how you can work with some of you.

00:08:44.633 --> 00:08:48.033
You'll see how quick and easy
you can get into the data,

00:08:48.233 --> 00:08:50.300
get the exploration, start
doing some analysis

00:08:50.300 --> 00:08:53.366
and start getting some meaning of the data
that you have in your movie.

00:08:54.466 --> 00:08:57.000
One of the things I truly love about
your movie

00:08:57.000 --> 00:09:00.500
is how easy it makes it to collaborate
with other people.

00:09:00.800 --> 00:09:02.266
And the reason it's easy is

00:09:02.266 --> 00:09:06.500
because when you share things with people,
you're sharing a single file.

00:09:06.500 --> 00:09:09.866
So for example, here's
a potential analysis that I might do

00:09:09.900 --> 00:09:12.800
when I'm working with somebody.
I've got a data set here.

00:09:12.833 --> 00:09:15.766
I've got the variables
that came with the dataset.

00:09:15.766 --> 00:09:18.200
I've got a calculated variable right here.

00:09:18.466 --> 00:09:22.800
You see the formula,
I've got a descriptive analysis.

00:09:22.800 --> 00:09:25.633
Let me put this over a little bit.

00:09:25.633 --> 00:09:30.066
I've got descriptive statistics,
I've got tables, I've got charts

00:09:30.066 --> 00:09:35.000
and box plots and all of this,
and it's saved in a single file.

00:09:35.000 --> 00:09:38.000
So let me show you this. For example,

00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:41.733
all of this is saved in a single file
that I have got called sharing

00:09:41.733 --> 00:09:46.300
files, dot OMV,
where dot OMV is the gem of the extension.

00:09:46.300 --> 00:09:49.666
When I work with somebody,
I do the analysis and then I send them

00:09:49.666 --> 00:09:53.566
the single file
and I tell them, download the movie

00:09:53.566 --> 00:09:56.500
double click on this
and you'll open up right to where we were.

00:09:57.366 --> 00:10:00.400
And they'll have all of the data,
all of the calculations,

00:10:00.400 --> 00:10:02.933
all the transformations,
all of the analyzes.

00:10:03.233 --> 00:10:05.566
They'll have everything
in one self-contained unit,

00:10:05.800 --> 00:10:08.800
and that is one of the easiest ways
to share things.

00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:10.466
You can email the file to them,

00:10:10.466 --> 00:10:14.166
you can put it in a shared folder
like Google Drive or Dropbox or Box.

00:10:14.666 --> 00:10:16.200
But anyway, you do it.

00:10:16.200 --> 00:10:19.800
The unitary nature of the files
and the fact that they have

00:10:19.833 --> 00:10:23.666
all the work that you've done in there
makes it extremely easy to collaborate

00:10:23.700 --> 00:10:25.766
and share work.

00:10:25.766 --> 00:10:30.400
G Movie makes it easy to share files
by simply emailing

00:10:30.400 --> 00:10:32.700
or putting in a shared box.

00:10:32.700 --> 00:10:35.233
The file that you've been working on
because it has the data,

00:10:35.233 --> 00:10:37.633
it has the transformation,
it has the analysis.

00:10:37.633 --> 00:10:42.200
There is, however, a really interesting,
more sophisticated alternative to that.

00:10:42.600 --> 00:10:44.900
And it's called
the Open Science Framework.

00:10:45.333 --> 00:10:49.500
And if you want to go here to OSX IO,
I can show you a little bit

00:10:49.500 --> 00:10:52.700
about how it works and how your movie
is very well adapted to it.

00:10:53.700 --> 00:10:55.033
The Open Science Framework

00:10:55.033 --> 00:11:00.500
is a free service that really facilitates
collaboration and reproducibility.

00:11:00.500 --> 00:11:01.566
In research.

00:11:01.566 --> 00:11:05.166
You can create a free account
and one of the important things here

00:11:05.466 --> 00:11:08.700
is that it allows you
to integrate a number of other services.

00:11:08.700 --> 00:11:12.133
So for instance, maybe you use Dropbox
or Google Drive or Box,

00:11:12.700 --> 00:11:14.433
it can bring files in from those.

00:11:14.433 --> 00:11:18.500
Or if you're the programmer type, you
might use GitHub or Amazon Web Services.

00:11:18.766 --> 00:11:20.266
Again, those are integrated.

00:11:20.266 --> 00:11:25.100
Now, I personally like to upload files
one by one into OSPF.

00:11:25.366 --> 00:11:27.300
That way
I have a better sense of what's there.

00:11:27.300 --> 00:11:30.000
I don't put something in a folder
by accident,

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:32.666
but this is a way to share your work.

00:11:32.666 --> 00:11:34.000
Both with specific other people

00:11:34.000 --> 00:11:37.400
for collaboration
and to share your results with the world.

