00:34:06.866 --> 00:34:10.066
But what I'm going to do here
is I'm going to change the variable type

00:34:10.066 --> 00:34:12.133
and I'm going
to change the names of the levels.

00:34:12.700 --> 00:34:15.566
Now it turns out that
when you have a small number

00:34:15.600 --> 00:34:18.466
of categories, they're one, two,
three, four, five is a small number.

00:34:18.900 --> 00:34:22.166
GMV assumes that it's a categorical
or nominal variable.

00:34:22.533 --> 00:34:24.700
The thing is,
it doesn't really make a difference

00:34:24.700 --> 00:34:27.933
if you're going to be averaging
or if you're going to be calculating

00:34:27.933 --> 00:34:30.900
statistics,
it'll do it on these variables.

00:34:31.433 --> 00:34:35.633
Even though they're defined as nominal,
you can change it

00:34:35.633 --> 00:34:38.266
to continuous or ordinal,
and that's going to affect

00:34:38.266 --> 00:34:42.233
the kinds of graphs that you can make
and is going to affect

00:34:42.766 --> 00:34:45.533
the ways that you can split the data.

00:34:46.033 --> 00:34:48.733
So it's not critical
if all you're going to do is average, but

00:34:48.733 --> 00:34:51.400
if you want to do other things with it,
it helps to define them.

00:34:52.300 --> 00:34:54.233
Now, a 1 to 5 rating scale,

00:34:55.333 --> 00:34:58.466
there's a debate
about what level of measurement it is.

00:34:58.500 --> 00:35:01.933
Technically, it's ordinal
because higher numbers indicate

00:35:02.100 --> 00:35:04.400
more agreement
or a higher evaluation of something

00:35:05.833 --> 00:35:09.133
on the other hand, in
every field I've ever seen, people

00:35:09.133 --> 00:35:10.766
actually take a 1 to 5 rating scale

00:35:10.766 --> 00:35:15.133
and treat it as though it were continuous
or quantitative so they can average it

00:35:15.600 --> 00:35:17.366
and so I'm actually going to come here
and I'm

00:35:17.366 --> 00:35:20.133
going to define this one as continuous.

00:35:20.400 --> 00:35:23.233
Now, you'll see here
that the levels went away

00:35:23.566 --> 00:35:26.600
because now it's treating these as,
you know,

00:35:26.600 --> 00:35:29.266
like time, 1 seconds to 5 seconds
doesn't have labels.

00:35:29.800 --> 00:35:33.900
So that's fine and I know what it means.

