01:09:17.466 --> 01:09:21.333
But you can see it does the same thing
where it colors them, it stacks them

01:09:21.333 --> 01:09:24.533
by the three different groups
and I'm going to turn off histogram

01:09:24.533 --> 01:09:26.966
so we can see what it looks like
without that.

01:09:26.966 --> 01:09:29.666
And this is where the pattern
really kind of comes into focus.

01:09:29.666 --> 01:09:32.933
We have the three different groups
and you can see the mounds are the piles

01:09:32.933 --> 01:09:36.866
of the data in ways that make it clear
that the distributions are basic

01:09:37.066 --> 01:09:40.066
you in a modal here,
but they're in different locations,

01:09:40.866 --> 01:09:44.566
a little bit different in skewness
it come down to pedal length

01:09:44.566 --> 01:09:49.133
and the differences become very clear
and I think that by using the smoothness

01:09:49.133 --> 01:09:53.633
of the density plot, it becomes
a little easier to see the overall pattern

01:09:53.633 --> 01:09:57.733
and not get distracted
by the jagged edges of the histograms.

01:09:57.733 --> 01:10:01.133
And so density plots
are another really helpful way to look

01:10:01.133 --> 01:10:04.200
at a quantitative or continuous variable,

01:10:04.800 --> 01:10:09.000
either for all data at once
or breaking it down by subgroups.

01:10:10.233 --> 01:10:11.133
The more you work with

01:10:11.133 --> 01:10:15.433
data, the more you learn that outliers
can be a really serious problem.

01:10:15.766 --> 01:10:19.433
They can throw off your analysis
and your conclusions dramatically.

01:10:19.800 --> 01:10:24.666
And so you want to pay special attention
to the presence of outliers in your data.

01:10:24.966 --> 01:10:28.200
And probably the easiest way to do
this is not with a histogram

01:10:28.200 --> 01:10:31.000
or with a density plot,
but with a box plot.

01:10:31.433 --> 01:10:34.333
So in this example,
which uses the iris data

01:10:34.333 --> 01:10:37.966
and starts up
where we left off with the density plots,

01:10:38.333 --> 01:10:42.866
I'm going to do box plots and show
how they relate to the density plots

01:10:43.166 --> 01:10:47.100
and the extra insight we can get from them
in terms of identifying outliers.

01:10:47.600 --> 01:10:51.400
Now to do this, I'm just going to click
on my existing density plot analysis,

01:10:52.600 --> 01:10:54.400
and then when that opens up,

01:10:54.400 --> 01:10:57.700
I'm going to come down here to plots

01:10:57.700 --> 01:10:59.266
and I'm going to click box plot.

01:10:59.266 --> 01:11:01.400
And you know,
this is in a separate column.

01:11:01.666 --> 01:11:04.733
And what this means
is it's going to produce a separate chart.

01:11:04.733 --> 01:11:06.833
So the density plots aren't to go away.

01:11:06.833 --> 01:11:10.066
This will be in addition,
but I'll then remove the density plots.

01:11:11.133 --> 01:11:14.166
So here come the box plots curl over.

01:11:14.166 --> 01:11:18.266
We'll see that the box is directly
underneath the density charts.

01:11:19.166 --> 01:11:20.966
And in the first one
there are no outliers.

01:11:20.966 --> 01:11:23.000
This one, we get a few outliers in each.

01:11:23.000 --> 01:11:26.266
And that's not surprising
because if you remember your statistical

01:11:26.266 --> 01:11:29.700
terminology, this is basically
a kleptocratic distribution,

01:11:30.533 --> 01:11:33.466
tends to have a lot of outliers
with people length.

01:11:33.966 --> 01:11:37.366
We don't have outliers,
but That's because of the bimodal nature.

01:11:37.400 --> 01:11:41.900
The middle part of the box
plot is spread out so much,

01:11:42.866 --> 01:11:43.566
and then the same

01:11:43.566 --> 01:11:47.466
thing is true here at the bottom again,
when it's spread out

01:11:47.466 --> 01:11:50.233
like that, that's, you know, something
really unusual is going on with your data.

01:11:50.633 --> 01:11:54.400
I'm going to turn off the density charts
in this analysis.

01:11:54.400 --> 01:11:56.666
And so we'll have just
the box plots at this point.

01:11:57.800 --> 01:12:00.000
Now, you can do the same thing

01:12:00.166 --> 01:12:03.233
with grouped analyzes
where you split it by something.

01:12:03.233 --> 01:12:07.166
So I'm going to close this analysis
for a moment and come down here

01:12:07.200 --> 01:12:10.633
where we had our stacked density plots

01:12:11.166 --> 01:12:14.366
showing the data
for the three different species.

01:12:14.366 --> 01:12:17.300
If I click on that,
I can do the same thing that I did.

01:12:17.300 --> 01:12:19.333
I can get a box plot,

01:12:19.366 --> 01:12:21.533
I'll click box plot,

01:12:21.800 --> 01:12:24.200
and it's going to show up
directly beneath each chart.

01:12:24.233 --> 01:12:28.633
Now this time we lose the pretty coloring,
but it's easy to see what's going on.

01:12:28.633 --> 01:12:33.833
We have an outlier for Virginia.

01:12:33.833 --> 01:12:36.833
We have two outliers,
each for citizen Virginia.

01:12:37.000 --> 01:12:38.700
You know, these are pretty symmetrical.

01:12:38.700 --> 01:12:40.500
They don't look bad.

01:12:40.500 --> 01:12:42.833
I'm going to come here
and turn off the density plots

01:12:43.533 --> 01:12:47.533
and so we can have just the box plots
and then we come down to Petal with you.

01:12:47.533 --> 01:12:51.866
We can see we got something really serious
going on here because this one is so

01:12:51.866 --> 01:12:57.233
squished, its distribution is so narrow
and it's so far away from the others

01:12:57.233 --> 01:12:59.933
that lets us know that we have something
really important going on.

01:13:00.333 --> 01:13:02.700
And then the paddle length, the same idea.

01:13:02.700 --> 01:13:05.500
And so box plots are a good way
of identifying outliers.

01:13:05.500 --> 01:13:08.700
We don't have any massive outliers
in this data set,

01:13:09.266 --> 01:13:11.933
which tells us
that we're in pretty good shape.

01:13:12.333 --> 01:13:15.700
But what we do have are strongly differing
distributions.

01:13:16.033 --> 01:13:17.366
On the other hand,
if you're doing something

01:13:17.366 --> 01:13:20.033
like the analysis of variance,
that's kind of what you're looking for.

01:13:20.333 --> 01:13:24.733
And this just confirms our insight
that the three species of iris

01:13:25.166 --> 01:13:27.533
differ significantly
in some of their measurements.

01:13:27.766 --> 01:13:29.900
The box plots are a good way
to check that.

01:13:29.900 --> 01:13:34.166
And again, a good way to check for the potential influence of outliers in the data

01:13:35.433 --> 01:13:36.000
jam movie

01:13:36.000 --> 01:13:39.800
includes one really kind of unusual,
kind of funny looking graph.

01:13:40.200 --> 01:13:42.700
It's called a violin and what it is

01:13:43.033 --> 01:13:47.066
and is sort of the box
plot version of the density plot,

01:13:47.333 --> 01:13:50.600
and it just kind of spreads out
and you'll see why it's called a violin.

01:13:50.933 --> 01:13:54.300
I'm starting with the iris data
set from the example data.

01:13:54.600 --> 01:13:57.933
And I'm using the box plots
that I created in the last video

01:13:58.400 --> 01:14:00.066
and simply going to click here

01:14:00.066 --> 01:14:03.366
where I have the four variables
and I'm not splitting them by anything.

01:14:04.000 --> 01:14:05.633
I can come here over the plots.

01:14:05.633 --> 01:14:08.133
And again, the reason violin's right here
is because

01:14:08.633 --> 01:14:11.633
Jamo is able to stack
all three of these on top of each other.

01:14:11.633 --> 01:14:14.100
You don't want to do that
because it gets really busy looking.

01:14:14.633 --> 01:14:17.333
But I'm going to show you the relationship
between the box spot

01:14:17.400 --> 01:14:20.100
and the violin plot
by simply adding it right here.

01:14:20.400 --> 01:14:21.000
Let do that.

01:14:21.000 --> 01:14:24.133
You get a shape that looks a little bit
like a Rorschach inkblot

01:14:24.733 --> 01:14:29.333
that actually corresponds
to what's happening in the box plot here.

01:14:29.333 --> 01:14:31.500
For instance, you can see that
we've got a lot of data here

01:14:31.500 --> 01:14:35.800
in the middle, and that's where the violin
plot goes out the most.

01:14:37.100 --> 01:14:38.666
We get a funny little manta ray

01:14:38.666 --> 01:14:42.166
shaped one here because of our strongly
kleptocratic distribution.

01:14:42.933 --> 01:14:45.600
And down here
you can see why it's called a violin plot,

01:14:45.600 --> 01:14:49.766
because we get it comes out, it goes back
in, comes out because of the bimodal

01:14:49.766 --> 01:14:52.400
distributions
that we have on these two variables.

01:14:52.800 --> 01:14:55.066
Now, I'm
actually going to remove the box plot

01:14:55.400 --> 01:14:57.800
so you can see
just the violin plot on its own.

01:14:58.466 --> 01:15:01.266
And what we're left with
is really just a little bit of squiggles.

01:15:01.266 --> 01:15:04.166
It feels like, again, a projective test.

01:15:04.166 --> 01:15:07.033
It's a little hard for me to read,
but it's an interesting version.

01:15:07.033 --> 01:15:08.933
Again, it's like a density plot,

01:15:08.933 --> 01:15:11.366
but oriented the same direction
as the box plot.

01:15:12.633 --> 01:15:17.466
You can, of course, do the same thing
with the subgroup analysis

01:15:17.466 --> 01:15:21.366
that we have down here where we broke
the measurements by species.

01:15:21.766 --> 01:15:24.100
I'm going to click on that
to open up this menu.

01:15:24.100 --> 01:15:26.400
And there you can see we have species
there.

01:15:26.400 --> 01:15:30.400
I'm going to click a violin and then I'm
actually going to also click a box plot.

01:15:30.400 --> 01:15:31.500
Right now,

01:15:31.633 --> 01:15:35.600
I know what we have feels like a little
set of drawing of ghosts or something,

01:15:35.900 --> 01:15:40.200
but you can see the distributions
for the three different species.

01:15:40.200 --> 01:15:43.833
And it really feels like
we're looking at a chart

01:15:43.833 --> 01:15:47.500
of animal shapes
as a nice little bat down here.

01:15:47.500 --> 01:15:50.800
But you can see this shows
that it's a strongly compressed range

01:15:50.800 --> 01:15:54.866
because again, the outcome variable
petal length, it goes vertically here

01:15:55.300 --> 01:15:58.433
and then petal with you can see again,
we have a very unusual distribution

01:15:58.433 --> 01:15:59.766
for this dataset.

01:15:59.766 --> 01:16:00.000
Well,

01:16:00.000 --> 01:16:03.600
things are almost kind of sort of normal
for the various of color and the Virginia.

01:16:03.866 --> 01:16:04.666
And so

01:16:04.666 --> 01:16:09.200
the violin plot, not a very common choice,
but potentially an interesting one.

01:16:09.200 --> 01:16:12.100
And it might be able to give you
some extra insight into your data

01:16:13.300 --> 01:16:14.200
box plots

01:16:14.200 --> 01:16:18.466
and violin plots are a nice way
of summarizing the distribution

01:16:18.466 --> 01:16:22.366
of a quantitative or continuous variable,
but maybe you actually want

01:16:22.366 --> 01:16:27.933
to see the data directly and dot plots
URL way that allow you to do that

01:16:28.366 --> 01:16:33.133
and with the violin plots that I made
using the iris data from the example set,

01:16:33.733 --> 01:16:38.233
and then I'm going to click
on the analysis to open up the commands

01:16:38.233 --> 01:16:41.466
and I'm going to come back down here
to plots and move this up.

01:16:41.466 --> 01:16:44.233
And what I'm going to do is simply add
data.

01:16:44.700 --> 01:16:47.766
Now, a lot of times
you don't want to have things stacked

01:16:47.766 --> 01:16:51.800
where you have more than one
kind of plot on top of another, the violin

01:16:51.800 --> 01:16:55.700
and the data, it works kind of
well because the violins an empty shape.

01:16:55.700 --> 01:16:57.466
And here you have the data in there.

01:16:57.466 --> 01:17:01.666
Now, what this is,
is every data point in the dataset.

01:17:01.666 --> 01:17:06.066
There's 150 in this particular case,
and they go from the lowest score

01:17:06.700 --> 01:17:08.866
up to the highest score.

01:17:09.133 --> 01:17:13.100
And you can see how they approximately
match the density of the violin plot.

01:17:13.600 --> 01:17:16.366
Now, this is called a jitter chart

01:17:16.366 --> 01:17:19.566
because every dot should be exactly
on the line here in the middle.

01:17:19.833 --> 01:17:22.566
But that makes it hard to tell
how many there are in a particular place

01:17:22.566 --> 01:17:25.866
because they might lay on
top of one another and so jittering is

01:17:25.866 --> 01:17:29.333
at random, only spreading them out
a little bit to the left, it to the right.

01:17:29.666 --> 01:17:31.866
So they're not usually
on top of each other

01:17:31.866 --> 01:17:36.133
and it's a little easier
to see the density of the distribution.

01:17:36.133 --> 01:17:37.500
I'll show you some others here.

01:17:39.300 --> 01:17:40.600
On the other hand,

01:17:40.600 --> 01:17:43.366
you have another choice
instead of just jittering.

01:17:43.366 --> 01:17:47.666
If you're a nice and orderly person,
you can also select stacked

01:17:49.066 --> 01:17:50.166
and what stack does.

01:17:50.166 --> 01:17:54.000
Instead of distributing them out randomly,
it puts them exactly where they need

01:17:54.000 --> 01:17:57.200
to go and arranges them out in mirror

01:17:57.200 --> 01:17:59.500
image patterns as you go up the chart.

01:18:00.733 --> 01:18:03.833
And so, again, this will appeal
to those of you who are very fastidious,

01:18:04.200 --> 01:18:09.366
but now you can see a little clearer
the distribution of points at each value.

01:18:09.966 --> 01:18:14.000
And you can see really how the violin plot
mimics that.

01:18:14.733 --> 01:18:17.033
I'm actually going to leave
violin plot on.

01:18:17.033 --> 01:18:20.333
Normally I would remove it
because the two go together nicely.

01:18:20.333 --> 01:18:22.433
They don't compete

01:18:22.500 --> 01:18:25.000
and then they're going to come down here
to where we have

01:18:25.000 --> 01:18:29.400
our same violin plots
but broken down by the species.

01:18:29.400 --> 01:18:31.700
I'm going to click on that
to bring up that analysis,

01:18:32.266 --> 01:18:36.066
and then I'll click on data
and I'll leave it stacked.

01:18:37.233 --> 01:18:40.300
Now, it's interesting, as I said, stacked,
but that appears to be jitter.

01:18:40.300 --> 01:18:41.933
So I'm going to see
if I can get his attention

01:18:41.933 --> 01:18:46.333
by switching back and forth from Girard
to Stack to if it changes anything.

01:18:47.033 --> 01:18:50.100
It was just a small glitch, but by going
back and forth, we straighten it out.

01:18:50.366 --> 01:18:54.800
And now you can see how the data are
arranged within each of the violin plots

01:18:55.800 --> 01:18:59.800
and the height
and the width of each of the violin plots.

01:18:59.800 --> 01:19:02.066
Now, it makes a little more sense.

01:19:02.066 --> 01:19:05.366
And so a dot plot of this kind, either
Girard

01:19:05.366 --> 01:19:08.666
or probably stacked, is a little easier
to read, lets

01:19:08.700 --> 01:19:13.066
you see what's going into the box plots
or into the violin plots

01:19:13.366 --> 01:19:18.666
unless you see the individual data points
that are going to drive your analysis.

01:19:19.833 --> 01:19:22.500
The last kind of chart I want to show you
an exploration

01:19:22.500 --> 01:19:26.633
is actually the simplest
and truthfully is often the most useful.

01:19:26.633 --> 01:19:28.800
It's just a bar chart.

01:19:28.800 --> 01:19:32.666
And all that does is it shows you
the number of cases in a category.

01:19:33.133 --> 01:19:36.033
Now, for this example,
I'm going to go back to the bugs data

01:19:36.733 --> 01:19:38.966
and this time
I'm only going to be focusing

01:19:39.000 --> 01:19:42.000
on the gender, region and education
variables.

01:19:42.000 --> 01:19:44.700
Those are the categorical or nominal ones.

01:19:44.700 --> 01:19:46.800
And I have frequencies
for each of those over here.

01:19:47.233 --> 01:19:50.500
First, I have the frequencies for each
variable without breaking them down.

01:19:50.800 --> 01:19:54.600
So I have the number of female
and male respondents,

01:19:54.600 --> 01:19:58.433
the frequencies for region where
most of the people are from North America

01:19:59.100 --> 01:20:00.900
and the frequencies for education.

01:20:00.900 --> 01:20:04.266
I'm going to click on this analysis
to bring up the menu.

01:20:04.266 --> 01:20:07.233
And here you can see how those three got
put over here.

01:20:07.233 --> 01:20:08.800
I turned off all the statistics.

01:20:08.800 --> 01:20:10.400
I turned on frequency table.

01:20:10.400 --> 01:20:12.033
That's how I got that.

01:20:12.033 --> 01:20:14.000
And now I'm going to do
just one little click here.

01:20:14.000 --> 01:20:17.466
I'm going to come and click bar chart
and you'll see that

01:20:17.733 --> 01:20:20.366
J movie knows to do that
automatically when you have

01:20:20.900 --> 01:20:24.833
nominal
that is categorical or ordinal variables.

01:20:24.833 --> 01:20:25.800
And that's what I have here.

01:20:25.800 --> 01:20:29.000
So I can click that
and then it's going to produce the bar

01:20:29.000 --> 01:20:33.633
charts directly below the tables
that I already have.

01:20:33.633 --> 01:20:35.400
You can see that it says Plot now

01:20:37.266 --> 01:20:38.133
and then it's easy to see.

01:20:38.133 --> 01:20:40.966
We have about twice as many women as men

01:20:41.700 --> 01:20:44.366
and we have an awful lot of people
from North America

01:20:44.900 --> 01:20:48.100
in this particular sample
and that the education

01:20:48.100 --> 01:20:51.133
is spread around
in kind of a peculiar way.

01:20:51.700 --> 01:20:55.900
But this is a good way of looking
at a categorical variable.

01:20:55.900 --> 01:20:58.000
It's so much easier
to get them from a bars

01:20:58.233 --> 01:21:00.466
and especially from bars,
because all they require

01:21:00.466 --> 01:21:04.100
is a relative linear judgment
as opposed to a pie chart too.

01:21:04.166 --> 01:21:05.466
Movie doesn't even do pie charts.

01:21:05.466 --> 01:21:07.800
That's
because they're often harder to read.

01:21:07.800 --> 01:21:10.933
So I'm going to close that one,
and then I'm going to come down

01:21:10.933 --> 01:21:12.766
to where I have the tables here.

01:21:12.766 --> 01:21:15.633
This is where I've broken it
down by gender.

01:21:15.633 --> 01:21:17.500
So let me click on this one.

01:21:17.500 --> 01:21:21.033
And you can see I'm getting a descriptive
analysis of education

01:21:21.033 --> 01:21:25.000
split by gender, and that's why
we have education down the side here.

01:21:25.300 --> 01:21:27.300
We have gender across the top.

01:21:27.300 --> 01:21:29.766
And I've turned off all the statistics.

01:21:29.766 --> 01:21:33.233
I have the frequency table on
and I do bar plot.

01:21:33.700 --> 01:21:37.200
And what this is going to do
is give a paired bar plot

01:21:37.200 --> 01:21:40.466
or grouped bar plot where it puts them
together for each group.

01:21:41.233 --> 01:21:43.733
I'm going to close this window here

01:21:43.733 --> 01:21:46.866
and then move this over a little bit
so you can see the whole thing.

01:21:47.566 --> 01:21:51.866
And now what we have
are our six different levels of education

01:21:51.866 --> 01:21:52.933
from advanced to some.

01:21:52.933 --> 01:21:55.100
Again, these are in alphabetical order.

01:21:55.100 --> 01:21:57.766
There's a natural order to these things
where more education

01:21:57.966 --> 01:22:02.033
should be further to the right and less
education should be further to the left.

01:22:02.033 --> 01:22:06.633
But we also have our gender
indicated by the colors.

01:22:06.633 --> 01:22:10.466
So for instance,
we have the reddish orange for female in

01:22:10.466 --> 01:22:14.233
sort of a teal for male and from
this is easy to see that

01:22:14.233 --> 01:22:19.133
we have a lot more women in each category
with the exception of partial

01:22:19.233 --> 01:22:23.033
over here where it's just
a couple of people in each in college.

01:22:23.333 --> 01:22:26.533
But the other ones have about a 2
to 1 ratio that we have overall.

01:22:26.800 --> 01:22:30.133
And so a bar chart and a grouped bar chart

01:22:30.766 --> 01:22:33.266
is a simple way of

01:22:33.266 --> 01:22:36.166
getting insight
from a categorical or nominal variable.

01:22:36.600 --> 01:22:39.833
Again, often the easiest,
the simplest kind of chart

01:22:40.200 --> 01:22:41.566
and really can be

01:22:41.566 --> 01:22:45.966
the most informative when you're trying
to get some quick insights from your data.

01:22:47.333 --> 01:22:50.000
So once you've conducted your analyzes
and you've created

01:22:50.000 --> 01:22:51.666
all these amazing visualizations,

01:22:51.666 --> 01:22:53.833
you want to be able to share them
