01:32:11.433 --> 01:32:14.433
In our next chapter on T tests into movie,

01:32:14.833 --> 01:32:18.000
we're going to go from that piece
to the whole.

01:32:18.000 --> 01:32:21.666
That is, we're going to start with
inferential statistics, which allows us

01:32:21.666 --> 01:32:25.433
to describe a sample of data
that we have in front of us

01:32:25.800 --> 01:32:30.333
and then use that to describe a population
something larger

01:32:30.333 --> 01:32:31.933
and more than what we observed.

01:32:31.933 --> 01:32:37.033
We are using the sample to infer things
about the population and the t test

01:32:37.033 --> 01:32:40.333
is the simplest and most direct way
to get started with this.

01:32:40.933 --> 01:32:44.400
In this chapter, we're going to talk about
three kinds of t tests.

01:32:44.800 --> 01:32:47.566
First, we'll look at the independent
samples, tests

01:32:47.566 --> 01:32:50.700
that you use to compare
two groups means to each other.

01:32:51.333 --> 01:32:54.466
The second is the paired samples
or repeated measures.

01:32:54.466 --> 01:32:59.266
T Test for you looking for changes
in scores from time one to time,

01:32:59.266 --> 01:33:04.066
two for one group of people,
and third is the one sample teachers

01:33:04.400 --> 01:33:09.600
where you're taking one samples
and comparing it to a known population.

01:33:09.600 --> 01:33:13.733
Mean taken together this three these serve
as an excellent introduction

01:33:13.733 --> 01:33:18.200
to the concept of inferential statistics
and going from the specifics

01:33:18.200 --> 01:33:22.966
of your sample at hand and generalizing
to the population at large,

01:33:24.366 --> 01:33:25.300
one of the simplest,

01:33:25.300 --> 01:33:29.166
inferential tests that you can do
is the independent samples t test.

01:33:29.400 --> 01:33:32.233
This is where you comparing
the means of two different groups.

01:33:32.533 --> 01:33:37.266
Very easy to do in a movie
and you can actually do several at a time,

01:33:37.266 --> 01:33:40.866
although there are going to be separate
comparisons to show you how this works.

01:33:40.866 --> 01:33:45.133
I'm using the example data set bugs,
which talks about how much people

01:33:45.133 --> 01:33:50.666
want to get rid of bugs that are either
low, disgusting, low fright,

01:33:50.700 --> 01:33:54.366
low discussing high fright
or through high disgust in high fright.

01:33:54.733 --> 01:33:58.033
And they try it on a zero
not at all to ten very much scale.

01:33:58.800 --> 01:34:02.533
And we have information about people's
level of education.

01:34:02.533 --> 01:34:06.566
The region they live
in, gender is coded as male and female.

01:34:06.566 --> 01:34:10.066
And so I'm going to use the gender one
and compare

01:34:10.066 --> 01:34:13.400
the male and female respondents
on these four different variables.

01:34:13.400 --> 01:34:17.266
Now, before you do a t test,
before we start doing inferential

01:34:17.266 --> 01:34:21.433
tests, it's a good idea to take a look
at the variables and to see how

01:34:21.433 --> 01:34:25.900
well they meet the assumptions,
because certain things like normality or

01:34:26.966 --> 01:34:29.533
similarity
and variance are important for a T test.

01:34:30.000 --> 01:34:35.066
So I'm going to come over here
to exploration and descriptive,

01:34:35.066 --> 01:34:37.666
and then I'm going to pick my four outcome
variables right here.

01:34:39.400 --> 01:34:41.700
I'll put them here under variables,

01:34:41.700 --> 01:34:45.200
and then I'm going to split
the whole thing by gender.

01:34:45.200 --> 01:34:46.433
It's not really the table

01:34:46.433 --> 01:34:48.700
that I'm most worried about,
although you can see that

01:34:48.700 --> 01:34:50.933
we have more female respondents
than males.

01:34:50.933 --> 01:34:55.800
It's not a big deal
and the means are pretty well.

01:34:55.800 --> 01:34:57.366
There's a one point difference.

01:34:57.366 --> 01:34:59.400
There's a two thirds,
there's a half point.

01:34:59.400 --> 01:35:01.633
So they vary.

01:35:01.633 --> 01:35:03.733
What I really want here are the plots.

01:35:03.766 --> 01:35:06.533
So I'm going to come over here to plots

01:35:06.533 --> 01:35:11.066
and I'm going to get a density histogram
or density chart

01:35:11.433 --> 01:35:15.766
and box plots
for each of these comparisons.

01:35:15.766 --> 01:35:19.466
And so what you see is these ones are kind
of sort of close to normal.

01:35:19.466 --> 01:35:21.633
These are female respondents here.

01:35:21.633 --> 01:35:23.266
These are male respondents here.

01:35:23.266 --> 01:35:25.233
Distributions are pretty similar.

01:35:25.233 --> 01:35:27.666
The box plots show
there's no outliers there.

01:35:27.666 --> 01:35:30.066
Actually, there's a lot of overlap.

01:35:30.066 --> 01:35:31.733
Okay, here's where we start getting non

01:35:31.733 --> 01:35:35.500
normal distributions,
which is in a sense a problem.

01:35:35.500 --> 01:35:38.400
But because of the central limit theorem
and because we're really working

01:35:38.400 --> 01:35:40.933
with sampling distributions, it's
not the end of the world.

01:35:40.933 --> 01:35:42.433
We can still go ahead and do things.

01:35:42.433 --> 01:35:46.500
We got a couple of outliers,
similar distributions.

01:35:48.066 --> 01:35:50.400
Okay, so now we see a little bit
what's going on.

01:35:50.400 --> 01:35:55.200
So with that as context,
I'm now going to do the regular T test

01:35:56.100 --> 01:35:58.400
and so I'm going to come over here
to T tests

01:35:59.200 --> 01:36:01.466
and do independent samples t test.

01:36:02.266 --> 01:36:05.166
And all I need to do
is pick my dependent variables

01:36:06.666 --> 01:36:08.866
or you can just call them
outcome variables

01:36:08.866 --> 01:36:12.266
and dependency for usually for
when it's a randomized experiment.

01:36:12.633 --> 01:36:15.333
And then the variable is the thing
that I want to split them

01:36:15.333 --> 01:36:16.366
into two different groups on.

01:36:16.366 --> 01:36:19.433
I'm going to use gender here,
so I'll click that over here

01:36:20.066 --> 01:36:25.000
and it gives us this table by default
and it's going to give us the T test

01:36:25.000 --> 01:36:28.266
and the P value,
which is for the significance test.

01:36:28.766 --> 01:36:32.233
And we can see from this right now
that actually there are no significant

01:36:32.233 --> 01:36:35.933
differences between the male
and female respondents on any of these.

01:36:36.333 --> 01:36:40.200
The closest we had was a P value .161.

01:36:40.666 --> 01:36:44.133
And the rule of thumb is
it needs to be less than .05

01:36:44.133 --> 01:36:47.000
to be considered
statistically significant.

01:36:47.000 --> 01:36:48.800
This one down here is a lot closer.

01:36:48.800 --> 01:36:51.000
It's 0.06, nearly significant.

01:36:51.000 --> 01:36:53.166
That's this last comparison right here.

01:36:55.033 --> 01:36:55.633
But there's a

01:36:55.633 --> 01:36:58.866
lot more you
can get from the T test function in GMV.

01:36:58.866 --> 01:37:01.233
And so I'm going to do
a few of these things

01:37:01.233 --> 01:37:03.866
now if you are familiar
with Bayesian statistics

01:37:04.000 --> 01:37:06.266
where we can incorporate that, it's
kind of nice.

01:37:06.733 --> 01:37:09.600
I'm going to come over here
and get the mean difference.

01:37:09.600 --> 01:37:14.600
So that's the mean of the women, minus
the mean of the men.

01:37:15.266 --> 01:37:20.166
And you can see they're about 2.55..606.

01:37:20.166 --> 01:37:21.900
Okay. I'm also going to get

01:37:21.900 --> 01:37:25.600
the confidence interval,
which by default is better than 95%.

01:37:25.800 --> 01:37:28.800
And if I scroll this over a little bit,
you can see the whole thing

01:37:29.700 --> 01:37:32.500
and also you can see that they're negative
on one side, positive on the other.

01:37:32.500 --> 01:37:34.400
So they include zero, which

01:37:34.400 --> 01:37:37.666
is consistent with the differences
not being statistically significant.

01:37:38.566 --> 01:37:40.666
I'm also going to get the effect size

01:37:42.033 --> 01:37:42.600
and this

01:37:42.600 --> 01:37:45.633
case it uses Cohen's D, which tells you

01:37:45.633 --> 01:37:49.600
how many standard deviations
there are between the two groups means.

01:37:50.133 --> 01:37:53.100
And in this case,
they're pretty small from close to zero

01:37:53.100 --> 01:37:56.233
to the biggest point
for standard deviations.

01:37:57.800 --> 01:38:00.833
I'm also going to get normality checks.

01:38:00.966 --> 01:38:04.566
So we know that the distributions
are not entirely

01:38:04.566 --> 01:38:07.133
normal, meaning bell curve shaped.

01:38:07.800 --> 01:38:11.533
And the test that we have
here is the SHAPIRO Wilk test.

01:38:11.533 --> 01:38:14.633
And it lets us know that really
none of them are exactly normal.

01:38:15.600 --> 01:38:17.000
We could tell that by looking at them.

01:38:17.000 --> 01:38:17.400
But again,

01:38:17.400 --> 01:38:18.866
it's really not the end of the world
because we're

01:38:18.866 --> 01:38:22.300
using the sampling distributions
and not the raw distribution.

01:38:22.300 --> 01:38:24.300
I'm also going to check
for equality of variances.

01:38:24.300 --> 01:38:26.566
That's something
that's also important for the T test.

01:38:26.566 --> 01:38:30.800
It says that the two groups
need to be spread out approximately the

01:38:30.800 --> 01:38:35.000
same amount, and this is going to use
Levine's test for the quality of variance.

01:38:35.000 --> 01:38:37.600
And you can see on this one,
none of these are significant.

01:38:37.600 --> 01:38:38.800
In fact, they're all really

01:38:40.133 --> 01:38:41.500
0.4 is the lowest.

01:38:41.500 --> 01:38:45.400
And so there's no significant difference
in the variability

01:38:45.400 --> 01:38:47.533
of the two distributions,
which is what we want.

01:38:48.166 --> 01:38:52.000
I'm also going to click descriptive

01:38:52.566 --> 01:38:53.300
and that's going to

01:38:53.300 --> 01:38:56.333
give me the means
and whatnot of each of the groups.

01:38:56.333 --> 01:38:58.366
And so you can see the mean, the median

01:38:58.366 --> 01:39:01.533
standard division and the standard error
which is used in the inferential tests.

01:39:02.266 --> 01:39:04.533
And then finally the descriptive plots.

01:39:04.533 --> 01:39:08.733
And in this case,
what it gives us are confidence intervals

01:39:08.733 --> 01:39:10.766
for the means as well.

01:39:10.766 --> 01:39:13.066
It shows the median for each group.

01:39:13.066 --> 01:39:16.200
And since these are
the ones that correspond most closely

01:39:16.533 --> 01:39:20.066
to the inferential test of the T test,
it's probably the best one for actually

01:39:20.266 --> 01:39:21.566
whether there's a difference.

01:39:21.566 --> 01:39:25.800
The general rule of thumb here
is that if the confidence interval

01:39:25.800 --> 01:39:29.800
that's a vertical line for a one group
overlaps with the mean

01:39:30.066 --> 01:39:34.166
the other group, then they're usually not
significantly different.

01:39:34.266 --> 01:39:36.700
And we've got a lot of overlap here,
a lot of overlap

01:39:37.200 --> 01:39:39.000
and these ones are pretty separate.

01:39:39.000 --> 01:39:41.400
And so this one was nearly significant.

01:39:41.400 --> 01:39:44.766
Anyhow, that's how you can do
the independent samples

01:39:44.766 --> 01:39:48.300
t test
using a single categorizing variable.

01:39:48.300 --> 01:39:51.666
In this case, I used male
and female respondents and you can use

01:39:51.666 --> 01:39:54.933
several outcome variables simultaneously.

01:39:55.200 --> 01:39:58.000
And it's an excellent
first step in getting a look

01:39:58.000 --> 01:40:04.366
at what's happening in your data
through inferential statistics.

01:40:04.366 --> 01:40:07.766
Sometimes you have data
from one group of people

01:40:07.800 --> 01:40:12.366
and you're comparing them either before
and after some kind of intervention or.

01:40:12.366 --> 01:40:14.666
You comparing them
on two separate measurements

01:40:14.700 --> 01:40:18.400
and you want to compare each person's
score with their own score.

01:40:18.733 --> 01:40:22.733
So you're looking for changes
from one variable or one time another.

01:40:23.033 --> 01:40:26.133
And this is when you want to use
a paired samples t test.

01:40:26.400 --> 01:40:28.733
And it's very easy to do here in Germany

01:40:28.733 --> 01:40:32.200
for this example,
I'm using the same bugs data talks

01:40:32.200 --> 01:40:37.166
about how much people want get rid of bugs
or insects that are low disgusting,

01:40:37.200 --> 01:40:40.500
low fried, up to high disgusting and high
frightening goes from zero,

01:40:40.500 --> 01:40:42.400
which is the lowest
to ten, which is the highest.

01:40:43.833 --> 01:40:44.466
I'm going to come

01:40:44.466 --> 01:40:47.566
up here and I'm going to use
paired samples t test.

01:40:47.566 --> 01:40:49.600
That's the test that we're doing here.

01:40:49.600 --> 01:40:53.433
And what we have to do
is we have to specify pairs of variables.

01:40:53.900 --> 01:40:57.300
Now, there are a few different ways
that you can select the pairs

01:40:57.300 --> 01:40:58.700
that you want to do.

01:40:58.700 --> 01:41:02.266
So, for instance, you can click
one variable and push it over here

01:41:02.266 --> 01:41:04.366
and then you simply click
the second variable.

01:41:04.366 --> 01:41:06.700
So maybe I'll do this one.

01:41:06.833 --> 01:41:09.233
And so that sets up a pair.

01:41:09.233 --> 01:41:14.733
You can also click one and do a command
or control click and select the other.

01:41:14.733 --> 01:41:17.900
So you get both of them there
at the same time and that gets a pair.

01:41:18.300 --> 01:41:23.133
Or if you have a bunch of compare scenes
all in a row that makes sense to do,

01:41:23.666 --> 01:41:26.966
you can select the whole thing
by just doing a shift click.

01:41:27.466 --> 01:41:32.633
And what is going to do is it's going
to put the variable all together in order.

01:41:32.900 --> 01:41:36.300
So number one goes with number two
and number three goes with number four

01:41:36.300 --> 01:41:37.300
and so on.

01:41:37.366 --> 01:41:40.166
Now what I have here, by the way, is

01:41:40.166 --> 01:41:43.766
a violation of the independence
of observations.

01:41:43.766 --> 01:41:47.133
Normally, you wouldn't want to be doing
the analysis quite like this

01:41:47.466 --> 01:41:51.700
because we're looking
at the same comparisons in different ways.

01:41:51.700 --> 01:41:54.900
This is more like a post hoc test
from an analysis of variance, but

01:41:55.800 --> 01:41:59.300
the procedures for doing this
are the same,

01:41:59.300 --> 01:42:03.533
and so it's a valid damage
titration of the process in your movie.

01:42:03.900 --> 01:42:05.700
So what I have right here

01:42:05.700 --> 01:42:09.166
are my four pairs of variables
and you can see that right over here.

01:42:09.733 --> 01:42:11.466
It's done all the tests already.

01:42:11.466 --> 01:42:16.233
We can see that, in fact, all four
comparisons are statistically significant.

01:42:16.233 --> 01:42:19.800
So in one sense, that's the simple thing
and we can stop right there.

01:42:20.033 --> 01:42:22.433
Fortunately,
J movie gives us some other options.

01:42:22.433 --> 01:42:24.600
I want to take a quick look at them.

01:42:24.600 --> 01:42:26.966
Let's look, for instance,
at the mean difference.

01:42:26.966 --> 01:42:28.466
How big is the difference?

01:42:29.633 --> 01:42:34.000
Or rather, we
also want to look at the mean difference.

01:42:34.000 --> 01:42:37.400
And what that does is
it gets the difference

01:42:37.733 --> 01:42:41.133
between the first variable
and the second variable for each person.

01:42:41.400 --> 01:42:45.100
That's a different score and then it gets
the average of that difference.

01:42:45.466 --> 01:42:49.433
And so here we have the differences
and you can see these ones went down

01:42:49.433 --> 01:42:50.700
across the comparison.

01:42:50.700 --> 01:42:52.933
One went up, by the way,
whether it's positive

01:42:52.933 --> 01:42:54.700
or negative is arbitrary,
is simply a matter

01:42:54.700 --> 01:42:57.000
of which of these you put first
and which one you put second.

01:42:58.000 --> 01:42:59.133
And we have the standard error.

01:42:59.133 --> 01:43:02.166
I'm also going to get the confidence
interval for the mean difference.

01:43:03.066 --> 01:43:05.700
I got to scroll over a little bit
so you can see that

01:43:06.566 --> 01:43:10.133
we'll get the effect size,
which is Cohen's D

01:43:10.133 --> 01:43:14.166
and in this case it says how many
standard deviations there are

01:43:15.233 --> 01:43:16.066
between

01:43:16.066 --> 01:43:19.200
these scores at time one and time
two or the scores on the first variable.

01:43:19.200 --> 01:43:20.433
On the second variable.

01:43:20.433 --> 01:43:24.900
Really what it is,
is the mean of the different scores

01:43:24.900 --> 01:43:27.333
divided by the standard deviation
of the different scores.

01:43:27.333 --> 01:43:29.233
And you can see
these ones are actually pretty big.

01:43:29.233 --> 01:43:32.266
That's almost a full standard deviation.

01:43:32.266 --> 01:43:34.766
We can also get descriptive,

01:43:34.766 --> 01:43:37.733
which gives us the means and the medians

01:43:37.733 --> 01:43:41.933
of the variables.

01:43:41.933 --> 01:43:46.266
We can do a normality check, which can be
important because t tests are valid

01:43:46.266 --> 01:43:50.633
when they're working with normal
or bell curve shape distributions.

01:43:50.633 --> 01:43:53.700
On the other hand, not really the raw
distribution that's important here,

01:43:53.700 --> 01:43:58.433
but the sampling distribution
and that's a slightly different thing.

01:43:58.666 --> 01:44:03.400
And even though have some statistically
significant things here, I usually don't

01:44:03.866 --> 01:44:06.966
personally worry about it very much
because it's the sampling distribution.

01:44:07.000 --> 01:44:09.400
As long as you have a large enough sample

01:44:09.400 --> 01:44:11.500
and we're pretty good,
you're probably okay.

01:44:11.800 --> 01:44:14.400
I'm going to finish
with the descriptive plots which

01:44:15.166 --> 01:44:18.566
give us confidence intervals
for the differences.

01:44:18.566 --> 01:44:21.133
So I'm going to wait a
second here while it updates

01:44:22.133 --> 01:44:24.366
and then
we'll scroll down here to the plots.

01:44:25.033 --> 01:44:28.200
And so what this shows us are the mean

01:44:28.800 --> 01:44:32.633
and the confidence interval for each of
the variables in the comparison.

01:44:32.633 --> 01:44:35.400
So this is low disgust,
low fright and high disgust. High fright.

01:44:35.400 --> 01:44:38.500
That's the biggest difference
available in the data.

01:44:38.500 --> 01:44:41.333
And you can see
they're very far away from each other.

01:44:41.333 --> 01:44:42.600
There's no overlap at all.

01:44:42.600 --> 01:44:44.100
The square is the median.

01:44:44.100 --> 01:44:46.200
And what's funny here is
you can see the median is actually

01:44:46.200 --> 01:44:50.000
above the confidence interval
because we had a ceiling effect there.

01:44:50.000 --> 01:44:53.800
People gave really high scores to that
and the means only lower

01:44:53.800 --> 01:44:57.133
because there were outliers on the low end

01:44:57.133 --> 01:45:00.600
and. You can see similar distributions
for some of the other ones.

01:45:00.966 --> 01:45:05.700
And so this is a good way of making
comparisons for the same group of people

01:45:06.200 --> 01:45:09.500
on one variable versus another where
they're looking at two different things.

01:45:09.500 --> 01:45:14.500
Or often when you're doing something before and after a particular intervention,

01:45:15.666 --> 01:45:16.400
the simplest

01:45:16.400 --> 01:45:19.700
version of T test
is the one sample T test,

01:45:19.933 --> 01:45:23.300
which allows you to compare
the mean of a single group

01:45:23.300 --> 01:45:27.400
that you have data from
to a chosen hypothesized value.

01:45:27.400 --> 01:45:30.500
It might be a national average,
it might be a theoretical value

01:45:30.500 --> 01:45:34.400
that represents the baseline against
which you want to compare everything.

01:45:34.800 --> 01:45:37.500
Now, this is really easy to do
in your movie here.

01:45:37.500 --> 01:45:40.400
I'm using the example dataset Big Five,

01:45:40.633 --> 01:45:45.600
which contains a number of ratings
where people evaluate themselves

01:45:45.600 --> 01:45:49.666
on five major personality characteristics
neuroticism,

01:45:49.666 --> 01:45:53.733
extroversion, openness to experience,
agreeableness and conscientiousness.

01:45:54.000 --> 01:45:56.733
And these are all rated on a 1 to 5 scale.

01:45:56.733 --> 01:46:00.133
And I want to show you how we can evaluate
all five of these simultaneously.

01:46:00.566 --> 01:46:04.400
But before you jump into T tests,
you always want to do

01:46:04.400 --> 01:46:08.800
some exploratory analysis
to see if your data are going to work

01:46:08.800 --> 01:46:11.933
well with the intended analysis.

01:46:12.200 --> 01:46:14.433
So let's go first over here to exploration

01:46:15.000 --> 01:46:18.766
and let's go to the descriptive.

01:46:18.766 --> 01:46:22.200
And what I'm going to do here
is I'm going to pick the five variables

01:46:23.100 --> 01:46:26.400
and put all of them
into the variable list.

01:46:26.400 --> 01:46:27.666
By the way,

01:46:27.666 --> 01:46:31.200
Jamovi updates frequently and in fact,

01:46:31.200 --> 01:46:31.766
from the time

01:46:31.766 --> 01:46:35.500
that I started this this morning
and I'm finishing it now, it's updated

01:46:35.500 --> 01:46:38.666
and we have a few extra options
available now under descriptive.

01:46:39.200 --> 01:46:43.833
So here we have the mean, the median,
the minimum and maximum by default.

01:46:44.633 --> 01:46:47.333
I also want to get the standard deviation

01:46:48.200 --> 01:46:52.200
and I may also want to get,
for instance, a normality test.

01:46:52.200 --> 01:46:54.100
And this is one of the new features.

01:46:54.100 --> 01:46:57.833
This is just a p value that lets us know
whether something is normal or not.

01:46:58.166 --> 01:47:00.500
And so this one is nearly not normal.

01:47:00.500 --> 01:47:03.600
This one's far away from normal
and so on and so forth.

01:47:04.533 --> 01:47:05.200
The important thing

01:47:05.200 --> 01:47:08.700
here actually is going to be the graphics
and I even have some more choices here.

01:47:09.033 --> 01:47:11.900
I'm going to do a density charts
like a histogram smoothed out.

01:47:12.133 --> 01:47:15.300
I'm also going to do box plots
which are good for identifying.

01:47:15.300 --> 01:47:17.233
And then I'm going to do the new option
here,

01:47:17.233 --> 01:47:20.766
which is a Q coupon,
which stands for Quantile Quantile Plot.

01:47:21.000 --> 01:47:25.466
And it shows how well the observed data
match up against, for instance,

01:47:25.466 --> 01:47:28.300
a normal distribution
that has the same mean

01:47:28.300 --> 01:47:30.100
and standard deviation
as the observed data.

01:47:30.100 --> 01:47:32.733
So I'm going to click that as well.

01:47:33.233 --> 01:47:34.500
And the charts look a little different.

01:47:34.500 --> 01:47:36.133
They're colored differently,
but here we have

01:47:36.133 --> 01:47:39.333
neuroticism that looks basically normal

01:47:40.133 --> 01:47:44.733
and the box plot shows that it's basically
symmetrical with the EU high outliers in

01:47:46.466 --> 01:47:47.900
the Q Q plot.

01:47:47.900 --> 01:47:51.800
It shows information on a diagonal,
and if all the dots were exactly on

01:47:51.800 --> 01:47:55.666
the diagonal,
that would tell you that the matches

01:47:55.666 --> 01:47:57.866
what you would expect
from a normal distribution.

01:47:57.866 --> 01:47:59.033
We're really close here. We have

01:47:59.033 --> 01:48:02.466
some small variations at the tails,
which is how it normally works.

01:48:03.133 --> 01:48:05.466
Extroversion,
you see, it's a little bit skewered.

01:48:05.466 --> 01:48:08.866
We've got some low outliers and you can
see that happening here at the bottom.

01:48:09.600 --> 01:48:11.466
Openness is symmetrical.

01:48:11.466 --> 01:48:13.300
It's slightly funny looking.

01:48:13.300 --> 01:48:15.300
You can see how it tapers
off here in here.

01:48:15.300 --> 01:48:17.633
But these are not major deviations.

01:48:17.633 --> 01:48:20.800
They're statistically significant
because it's a large sample here.

01:48:20.800 --> 01:48:23.200
We have some low outliers on agreeableness

01:48:23.200 --> 01:48:25.500
because most people want to say
that they're really agreeable

01:48:27.066 --> 01:48:30.666
and then conscientiousness,
same general idea.

01:48:30.900 --> 01:48:33.000
And so that's
some of the important background.

01:48:33.000 --> 01:48:36.433
And what I saw here
is nothing really worrisome.

01:48:36.733 --> 01:48:39.466
And this is just confirmation
that our data

01:48:39.466 --> 01:48:42.933
probably work well with the assumptions
of a one sample t test,

01:48:43.200 --> 01:48:48.000
which includes normality,
and we can go ahead and do our analysis.

01:48:48.333 --> 01:48:53.400
So now I'm going to come over here
to t tests and click on one sample t test

01:48:54.633 --> 01:48:57.433
and when
I do that, I can select my variables.

01:48:57.433 --> 01:49:00.566
I'm actually going to select
all five of them simultaneously.

01:49:00.866 --> 01:49:04.333
You only want to select multiple variables
if they have

01:49:04.533 --> 01:49:07.500
the same null value
that you're comparing them to.

01:49:07.800 --> 01:49:10.100
Now, a lot of times that no value
is going to be zero.

01:49:10.400 --> 01:49:12.566
That's a silly thing to do in this case

01:49:12.566 --> 01:49:16.300
because zero is not even in the scale
these are rated on a 1 to 5 scale.

01:49:16.700 --> 01:49:19.466
And so what I'm going to do
is I'm going to come down here and I'm

01:49:19.466 --> 01:49:23.666
going to change the test value from 0 to 3

01:49:24.100 --> 01:49:26.600
because that's the midpoint of a
1 to 5 scale.

01:49:27.300 --> 01:49:30.166
And when I do that,
you see how this table over here updates

01:49:30.500 --> 01:49:32.200
it's doing student's t test.

01:49:32.200 --> 01:49:34.666
It's the one sample T test
and can see that

01:49:34.666 --> 01:49:36.900
all of them the mean is
significantly different from three.

01:49:37.100 --> 01:49:39.300
This is not a surprise
because most of these

01:49:39.300 --> 01:49:42.700
there's a positive
or a desirable end of the scale.

01:49:43.033 --> 01:49:46.766
People to be agreeable,
they want to be open.

01:49:47.200 --> 01:49:49.966
And so we tend to have high values.

01:49:49.966 --> 01:49:53.500
But I'm also going to look
at the mean difference how far away are

01:49:53.500 --> 01:49:57.500
each of these variables means
from the hypothesized value of three?

01:49:58.066 --> 01:50:00.900
And you can see
they're not really big differences.

01:50:00.900 --> 01:50:04.200
You know, 2/10 of a point,
about six of a point.

01:50:04.800 --> 01:50:08.500
We're going to get the effect size
for each one of these, which compares

01:50:08.666 --> 01:50:12.900
how far the mean is of the sample data
to the population.

01:50:12.900 --> 01:50:15.300
Mean divided by the standard deviation.

01:50:15.300 --> 01:50:19.366
And here because the standard deviations
are not big, this value of Cohen's

