02:06:31.733 --> 02:06:35.533
Any time you're analyzing data,
you are making comparisons.

02:06:35.766 --> 02:06:39.500
You may be making them explicitly
or you may be making them implicitly.

02:06:40.166 --> 02:06:41.200
They comparison.

02:06:41.200 --> 02:06:45.300
That's usually most important
in research is, for instance,

02:06:45.600 --> 02:06:50.166
a control or baseline condition versus
some sort of experimental or manipulated

02:06:50.166 --> 02:06:51.866
or intervention condition.

02:06:52.800 --> 02:06:54.000
The idea is that if

02:06:54.000 --> 02:06:56.833
people are randomly assigned
to one condition or another,

02:06:57.033 --> 02:06:59.300
then the differences
between people balance out.

02:06:59.300 --> 02:07:01.500
And you can look the effect
of just that manipulation.

02:07:01.833 --> 02:07:04.500
But a really interesting and powerful

02:07:04.500 --> 02:07:08.200
alternative to this and powerful meaning
statistically powerful.

02:07:08.233 --> 02:07:10.566
Easier to find the effect fewer people

02:07:11.200 --> 02:07:13.433
is what's called
a repeated measures design.

02:07:13.666 --> 02:07:15.600
And this is where everybody in your study

02:07:15.600 --> 02:07:19.833
gets to serve as their own control,
their own comparison or baseline.

02:07:19.833 --> 02:07:24.300
No way you do that is by gathering data
from them in more than one condition.

02:07:24.300 --> 02:07:27.600
If you have four conditions,
you get it in all four conditions,

02:07:27.866 --> 02:07:31.800
and then you compare the change
one condition to another for each person.

02:07:32.100 --> 02:07:36.466
Again, a much more powerful design
if your study allows for it.

