02:59:50.400 --> 02:59:53.166
do linear regression
and what we're going to do

02:59:53.666 --> 02:59:57.466
is a we're going to pick open this
as our dependent variable.

02:59:58.166 --> 03:00:00.800
It isn't really a dependent variable
because it's not

03:00:00.800 --> 03:00:03.900
from a manipulated experiment,
but it's the outcome variable.

03:00:03.900 --> 03:00:05.633
And the idea here
is that the outcome scores

03:00:05.633 --> 03:00:08.400
depend on the other variables
we put into the model.

03:00:08.766 --> 03:00:09.933
So let's take openness.

03:00:09.933 --> 03:00:12.533
Now I have a small data set.

03:00:12.533 --> 03:00:17.066
There's only 48 cases in this
because it's the contiguous United States.

03:00:17.833 --> 03:00:20.300
And so I can't go hog
wild and throw everything in there

03:00:20.300 --> 03:00:23.833
because I'm going to violate
some of the assumptions of regression.

03:00:23.833 --> 03:00:25.866
So I'm going to be a little bit selective.

03:00:25.866 --> 03:00:27.033
Just a little bit.

03:00:27.033 --> 03:00:29.233
It's not going to put everything in there.

03:00:29.233 --> 03:00:32.833
I'm going to pass up some of these things.

03:00:33.266 --> 03:00:34.866
I'm going to leave these other

03:00:34.866 --> 03:00:36.966
big five personality
characteristics right here

03:00:36.966 --> 03:00:39.300
because they're not supposed
to be associated.

03:00:39.300 --> 03:00:41.200
But I am going to come and get

03:00:42.966 --> 03:00:44.400
Google searches.

03:00:44.400 --> 03:00:48.300
And again, what it tells us is
what is the state's average on

03:00:48.300 --> 03:00:52.466
this as a proportion
of their total number of searches

03:00:52.700 --> 03:00:56.133
and it's given as Z scores
compared to the rest of the United States,

03:00:56.433 --> 03:00:59.266
trying to select all of those
and put them over here in the covariates.

03:01:00.966 --> 03:01:04.666
And actually, I, I will take one of these
categorical variables.

03:01:04.666 --> 03:01:05.033
I'll get.

03:01:05.033 --> 03:01:09.100
Governor, this is a dichotomous variable
in this particular dataset.

03:01:09.100 --> 03:01:12.033
It only has two values Democratic
or Republican.

03:01:12.333 --> 03:01:16.000
There actually is an independent governor
right now, but he's up in Alaska.

03:01:16.400 --> 03:01:19.800
And Alaska wasn't included in this dataset
because we don't have the psychological

03:01:19.800 --> 03:01:21.266
information for them.

03:01:21.266 --> 03:01:23.666
So we can treat it as dichotomous.

03:01:23.666 --> 03:01:27.933
And you put that here under factor
and you can see that off to the right.

03:01:27.933 --> 03:01:31.700
The regression
table has filled in immediately.

03:01:32.533 --> 03:01:33.966
We can tell a couple of things here.

03:01:33.966 --> 03:01:38.700
First off, the multiple correlation,
the big R here is 0.831.

03:01:39.100 --> 03:01:42.700
Again, correlation goes from negative
one to positive one.

03:01:43.200 --> 03:01:46.933
And this is a strong correlation
with multiple regression

03:01:46.933 --> 03:01:48.366
like we have right here.

03:01:48.366 --> 03:01:51.600
It's more common to look at the R squared,
actually, the adjusted R squared.

03:01:51.600 --> 03:01:53.000
I'll show you in a minute.

03:01:53.100 --> 03:01:55.433
But this can be interpreted
as the proportion of variance

03:01:55.433 --> 03:01:58.700
in the outcome openness
that's predicted by these other variables

03:01:58.700 --> 03:02:02.400
in this 69%, which is truly kind of huge.

03:02:02.900 --> 03:02:09.000
And if we want to, we can come down here
and look at these individual variables

03:02:09.000 --> 03:02:11.300
and how they contribute now keep in mind

03:02:12.000 --> 03:02:16.500
these coefficients, these are the things
that you multiply each number by

03:02:17.033 --> 03:02:21.333
are only valid
when you take this set variables together.

03:02:21.766 --> 03:02:25.733
This is not
how much is associated on its own.

03:02:26.133 --> 03:02:28.366
This is as a whole.

03:02:28.800 --> 03:02:31.466
So if I were to drop
one of these variables out or add

03:02:31.466 --> 03:02:33.133
another one
in, all these numbers would add

03:02:33.133 --> 03:02:35.766
just a little bit
because it's the unity here.

03:02:35.766 --> 03:02:37.033
This is important.

03:02:37.266 --> 03:02:42.000
That said, we can tell that some of these
seem to matter more than others.

03:02:42.000 --> 03:02:44.800
We're looking for p values
that are less than oh five

03:02:45.300 --> 03:02:50.300
and we're not getting much here
on any of these until we get ten.

03:02:50.300 --> 03:02:51.100
Here at the bottom

03:02:52.066 --> 03:02:54.933
scrapbook,
this is how much does that state

03:02:54.933 --> 03:02:59.133
search for the term scrapbook
compared to other states?

03:02:59.133 --> 03:03:01.066
And that makes a difference.

03:03:01.066 --> 03:03:05.233
States where scrapbooking is more common
of a lower

03:03:05.400 --> 03:03:09.933
level of openness, at least when taken
in the context of this entire set of data.

03:03:10.266 --> 03:03:12.200
And then searches for modern dance

03:03:12.200 --> 03:03:16.166
are in fact significantly associated
with higher levels of openness.

03:03:16.500 --> 03:03:20.600
Again, within the context
of this set of variables.

03:03:20.966 --> 03:03:25.766
And then down here gov is a dichotomous
variable that takes two values.

03:03:26.066 --> 03:03:29.600
And the nice thing about your movie,
it's able to simply take

03:03:29.600 --> 03:03:31.833
a categorical variable
and throw it in there

03:03:31.833 --> 03:03:35.200
and prepare it to work
well in a regression.

03:03:35.700 --> 03:03:37.500
On the other hand,
we see that this one is nowhere

03:03:37.500 --> 03:03:40.333
close to statistically significant,
so we don't really need to worry about it.

03:03:40.766 --> 03:03:41.800
But let's come over here

03:03:41.800 --> 03:03:45.233
and see what some of the options
are for linear regression in J Movie.

03:03:46.066 --> 03:03:48.233
Scroll up here a little.

03:03:48.233 --> 03:03:51.933
Let's go to Model Builder and this is
if I wanted to put the information

03:03:51.933 --> 03:03:55.733
in in blocks,
that actually might be a good thing.

03:03:56.100 --> 03:03:57.533
In fact, let's try that.

03:03:57.533 --> 03:04:03.000
Let's do blocks where I'm going to take
all of these variables out right here

03:04:03.600 --> 03:04:06.233
and I'm going to make the first block,
just the governor,

03:04:06.233 --> 03:04:08.666
whether that person's Democrat
or Republican.

03:04:09.300 --> 03:04:12.766
And then I will add a second block

