03:06:45.100 --> 03:06:48.233
By the way, you may notice
we have the option

03:06:48.233 --> 03:06:51.600
of variables in blocks,
block one and block two.

03:06:51.600 --> 03:06:52.233
BLOCK three.

03:06:52.233 --> 03:06:53.633
That actually is a good way to go.

03:06:53.633 --> 03:06:57.600
If you want to look at the separate
or additional

03:06:57.933 --> 03:07:00.400
predictive ability of one variable
or another,

03:07:01.166 --> 03:07:05.066
one thing you do not get to do in GMV
and some people say

03:07:05.066 --> 03:07:09.333
this is a very good thing is you can't do
step variable selection.

03:07:09.666 --> 03:07:12.166
You can't throw a whole bunch of variables
at it and say, look,

03:07:12.300 --> 03:07:14.700
you just sort through it
all on your own and tell me what works.

03:07:15.633 --> 03:07:16.633
That's an easy

03:07:16.633 --> 03:07:20.466
way to do things,
but It tends to really magnify

03:07:20.700 --> 03:07:25.033
the result of weird
flukey results in the data,

03:07:25.033 --> 03:07:27.566
and you tend to get results
that don't generalize.

03:07:27.566 --> 03:07:30.600
Well.
And so that's not even an option here.

03:07:30.900 --> 03:07:34.700
You can, however, do blocks
where you choose

03:07:34.700 --> 03:07:37.900
what goes in at one point
and you examine what's happening.

03:07:37.900 --> 03:07:41.066
I actually prefer blocked entry like this

03:07:42.033 --> 03:07:45.200
for reference level that's for categorical
or nominal variables

03:07:45.200 --> 03:07:48.633
and you get to decide
which category is baseline one

03:07:48.633 --> 03:07:51.566
and which one gets the regression
coefficient that goes with it.

03:07:52.866 --> 03:07:56.300
Right now it's Democrat is the baseline
and Republican goes on top.

03:07:56.300 --> 03:07:58.166
I can just flip around

03:07:58.166 --> 03:08:01.400
and then you can see that
this will switch over here to the end.

03:08:01.400 --> 03:08:08.333
It'll just change the sign associated
with it so it doesn't change anything big.

03:08:08.333 --> 03:08:09.566
Well, that's refreshing.

03:08:09.566 --> 03:08:12.133
I'll close this and come to assumption
checks.

03:08:12.133 --> 03:08:13.066
There's a number of things

03:08:13.066 --> 03:08:17.633
that need to be true for the results
of linear regression to be meaningful.

03:08:17.633 --> 03:08:21.866
Now, autocorrelation is if you had stuff
like week one, week to week three

03:08:22.133 --> 03:08:24.933
where the week three results,
you know, are going to be at least

03:08:24.933 --> 03:08:27.000
partially associated with week one.

03:08:27.000 --> 03:08:30.133
We're not dealing with that,
but we are going to be dealing with

03:08:30.133 --> 03:08:35.733
curling reality where the predictor
variables are associated among themselves

03:08:36.166 --> 03:08:39.966
and what causes the associations to change

03:08:39.966 --> 03:08:43.433
dramatically when variables
are in different combinations.

03:08:43.733 --> 03:08:47.100
If no variable was associated
with any other, it wouldn't matter

03:08:47.100 --> 03:08:49.533
the order that you put them in
or whether they were in there

03:08:50.433 --> 03:08:51.766
with the others or not.

03:08:51.766 --> 03:08:54.666
It would always be consistent,
but because these variables

03:08:54.666 --> 03:08:58.666
are associated, it becomes very sensitive
to the way that you enter them.

03:08:59.133 --> 03:09:03.166
Let's roll down a little bit and let's
look at two colony already statistics.

03:09:03.166 --> 03:09:06.500
The first is via F,
which stands for variance Inflation

03:09:06.500 --> 03:09:09.566
Factor, and the second is tolerance.

03:09:10.066 --> 03:09:12.900
And you can take the two of these

03:09:12.900 --> 03:09:15.700
as an indication of how associated

03:09:16.200 --> 03:09:19.200
each of these variables
is with all of the others.

03:09:19.466 --> 03:09:22.400
Now, I can tell by looking at this
that we do have some

03:09:22.966 --> 03:09:24.933
CO linearity or multiple linearity.

03:09:24.933 --> 03:09:28.066
So between them
it's not the end of the world.

03:09:28.066 --> 03:09:30.200
I'm going to go past it.

03:09:30.200 --> 03:09:33.733
We can also look at something
like the Q Q plot of residuals.

03:09:33.733 --> 03:09:36.600
That's the quantile.
Quantile or plot of residuals.

03:09:37.633 --> 03:09:38.233
When you do

03:09:38.233 --> 03:09:41.333
a regression, the residual is the distance

03:09:41.333 --> 03:09:44.733
between the predicted values
you give people and their actual values.

03:09:44.733 --> 03:09:48.400
It's the error, the leftover part,
and in theory

03:09:48.400 --> 03:09:51.766
that should be normally distributed.

03:09:52.066 --> 03:09:55.633
You shouldn't always be higher,
always be low, should be spread out

03:09:56.533 --> 03:09:58.866
basically like a bell curve.

03:09:58.866 --> 03:10:01.066
And if our residuals were

03:10:01.066 --> 03:10:04.266
perfectly shaped like a bell curve,
they'd all be exactly on this line.

03:10:04.266 --> 03:10:05.600
But you can see they're really close.

03:10:05.600 --> 03:10:07.966
So that part is nice.

03:10:07.966 --> 03:10:09.700
You can get separate residual plots.

03:10:09.700 --> 03:10:12.400
It'll do one for every one
of the variables that you put in there.

03:10:12.400 --> 03:10:15.000
You can also get Cook's distance,
which is a way of looking at

03:10:15.833 --> 03:10:18.533
how influential a single data point is.

03:10:19.200 --> 03:10:21.900
I'm going to skip over those
and come down to model fit.

03:10:22.500 --> 03:10:26.233
We have R and R squared
and if I come back up

03:10:26.233 --> 03:10:29.266
here, you'll see where those things go.

03:10:30.366 --> 03:10:31.466
Adjusted R squared

03:10:31.466 --> 03:10:31.933
is probably going

03:10:31.933 --> 03:10:35.400
to be a better choice in this situation,
especially because we have a small

03:10:35.400 --> 03:10:35.833
data set.

03:10:35.833 --> 03:10:39.733
There's only 48 cases
and you can see that there is a change.

03:10:39.900 --> 03:10:45.000
The R squared is 69
and the adjusted R squared is 572.

03:10:45.000 --> 03:10:48.800
This is going to be a more accurate number
in terms of generalizing

03:10:48.800 --> 03:10:50.700
to other data sets.

03:10:50.700 --> 03:10:53.933
We have other information
we could use like the AIC

03:10:53.933 --> 03:10:57.966
that's, the key information criteria
or the Bayesian information criteria.

03:10:58.666 --> 03:11:01.033
We can also do the overall F test.

03:11:01.033 --> 03:11:03.866
Let me click on that
and you'll see how it expands

03:11:04.000 --> 03:11:06.600
some of the information
that we have in here.

03:11:06.600 --> 03:11:12.000
That is, it adds a few more columns here
where we're doing the overall model.

03:11:12.000 --> 03:11:14.200
Now we have two different models.
We have two entries.

03:11:14.200 --> 03:11:17.866
When it was just the governor,
we had a highly significant result.

03:11:18.233 --> 03:11:19.866
And when we added on all that Google

03:11:19.866 --> 03:11:24.933
search information,
we got an even stronger result here.

03:11:24.933 --> 03:11:25.833
In terms of model

03:11:25.833 --> 03:11:29.700
coefficients, one thing you can do is
you can do the omnibus F test.

03:11:29.700 --> 03:11:30.766
We've basically done that.

03:11:31.733 --> 03:11:34.833
But what I would like
to do is these standardized estimates.

03:11:34.833 --> 03:11:39.233
So instead of T tests
here, we'll actually get Z scores

03:11:39.233 --> 03:11:42.633
where we compare the regression
coefficients against each other

03:11:42.633 --> 03:11:45.833
and can even get a confidence
interval, 95% confidence interval

03:11:45.833 --> 03:11:49.533
for those two over just a little bit.

03:11:50.200 --> 03:11:52.633
And the standardized estimate,
these are Z scores

03:11:52.633 --> 03:11:56.133
for each of these variables over here
as opposed to TS.

03:11:56.466 --> 03:11:58.466
And then we have confidence intervals
for each of them.

03:11:58.466 --> 03:12:02.066
And you can see, for instance,
that we have one here that is going to be

03:12:02.400 --> 03:12:06.200
the modern dance, one where the two sides
of the confidence interval

03:12:06.200 --> 03:12:06.966
have the same sign.

03:12:06.966 --> 03:12:13.066
They're both positive, which corresponds
with it being statistically significant.

03:12:13.066 --> 03:12:16.566
And then finally you can get estimated
marginal means.

03:12:17.200 --> 03:12:20.366
So for instance, we can come down here
and we can put

03:12:21.266 --> 03:12:23.866
Governor
one of these, we can put it right there,

03:12:24.833 --> 03:12:25.800
and that will produce

03:12:25.800 --> 03:12:27.833
a new chart down here at the bottom

03:12:29.766 --> 03:12:31.066
and well, that working on it.

03:12:31.066 --> 03:12:33.666
I'm going to add another near term.

03:12:33.666 --> 03:12:37.066
Let's say, for instance,
let's put modern dance in there

03:12:38.766 --> 03:12:40.866
and you do this for each of them
if you want

03:12:42.600 --> 03:12:46.666
to. Showing us is the level of openness
for the state broken down

03:12:46.666 --> 03:12:49.733
by whether that state currently
has a Republican or Democrat governor?

03:12:50.200 --> 03:12:54.200
And you can see that there's an enormous
amount of overlap between those two.

03:12:54.800 --> 03:12:57.900
And then this is the more fun one
when you take something

03:12:57.900 --> 03:13:00.466
that is on a quantitative scale,

03:13:01.000 --> 03:13:04.400
you get sort of this regression
line in terms of how open people are.

03:13:04.400 --> 03:13:08.266
And this is the standard error estimate
or a confidence interval

03:13:08.266 --> 03:13:09.600
for the regression line.

03:13:09.600 --> 03:13:13.366
So you can see that as modern dance
becomes more common as a search term,

03:13:13.800 --> 03:13:16.233
that the level of openness for the state
goes up as well.

03:13:16.533 --> 03:13:19.600
And again, you can do that
for one of these other variables.

03:13:19.600 --> 03:13:21.600
It's just going to be an enormous amount.

03:13:21.600 --> 03:13:25.500
But the general idea here is that
this is the set of tools

03:13:25.500 --> 03:13:29.166
that your movie gives you for doing
linear regression, which is probably

03:13:29.166 --> 03:13:32.666
the single most important
analytical procedure

03:13:32.966 --> 03:13:34.866
you can have
when you're working with your data.

