03:14:11.633 --> 03:14:14.366
And to do that, I'm
going to come back to regression

03:14:15.400 --> 03:14:15.966
and I'm going

03:14:15.966 --> 03:14:20.233
to pick linear regression again
and I'm going to set up a new analysis.

03:14:20.233 --> 03:14:22.500
I mean, I scroll down a little bit here.

03:14:22.500 --> 03:14:25.600
The outcome variable,
the dependent is openness.

03:14:25.600 --> 03:14:28.133
So I'm going to pick that right here.

03:14:28.133 --> 03:14:30.866
And this is where I can start choosing

03:14:30.866 --> 03:14:33.866
which variables
I want to include in the analysis.

03:14:34.333 --> 03:14:35.900
Now, what I did

03:14:35.900 --> 03:14:40.700
is I picked all of these Instagram down
through modern dance,

03:14:40.700 --> 03:14:44.300
which are Google Correlate terms,
which have to do with the relative

03:14:44.300 --> 03:14:47.666
popularity of these search terms
on a state by state basis.

03:14:48.866 --> 03:14:49.933
And I can pick those.

03:14:49.933 --> 03:14:53.766
And I also chose gov,
which is simply whether

03:14:54.066 --> 03:14:58.933
the Governor of that state was Democratic
or Republican when the data was gathered

03:14:59.400 --> 03:15:02.800
and we have this table right here,
but what I'm going to do is I'm going

03:15:02.800 --> 03:15:04.866
to come down here to model

03:15:06.300 --> 03:15:06.900
and here

03:15:06.900 --> 03:15:10.800
I have an opportunity
to do data in blocks.

03:15:11.100 --> 03:15:14.133
Now, a very common approach or something
that a lot of people want to do

03:15:14.133 --> 03:15:19.166
is something called entry,
which you simply tell the computer,

03:15:19.166 --> 03:15:20.700
these are all the possible variables

03:15:20.700 --> 03:15:23.266
and I want you the computer
to go through and pick

03:15:23.266 --> 03:15:25.500
the one that has the highest
individual correlation.

03:15:25.500 --> 03:15:26.900
Stick that in the model,

03:15:26.900 --> 03:15:28.500
then pick
the one that has the highest correlation

03:15:28.500 --> 03:15:30.666
after that and put it in and so on
and so forth.

03:15:31.300 --> 03:15:36.200
The problem is stepwise models
tend to really build on the idiosyncrasies

03:15:36.200 --> 03:15:37.633
of that particular data set.

03:15:37.633 --> 03:15:40.433
And so you get a model that's really well
tailored to what you have,

03:15:40.666 --> 03:15:43.466
but it doesn't generalize well
and it can actually mislead

03:15:43.466 --> 03:15:45.000
you in a lot of different ways.

03:15:45.000 --> 03:15:47.366
Jamovi doesn't
even give you the option for that.

03:15:47.866 --> 03:15:50.500
What you do have the option
for is what I personally call

03:15:50.500 --> 03:15:54.300
block regression
or block entry or block wise regression.

03:15:54.300 --> 03:15:58.400
And that is you can set up several models
that sequentially

03:15:58.733 --> 03:16:00.833
add additional variables.

03:16:01.166 --> 03:16:02.233
And so what I'm going to do

03:16:02.233 --> 03:16:06.366
here is I'm going to first
take all of these search terms.

03:16:06.366 --> 03:16:07.933
I'm going to get all of them out.

03:16:08.900 --> 03:16:09.933
And so right now.

03:16:09.933 --> 03:16:15.000
BLOCK One is just whether the state
has a Democratic or Republican governor.

03:16:15.600 --> 03:16:19.000
And you can see right here
that in terms of openness, it's

03:16:19.000 --> 03:16:21.400
doing subtraction,
Republican minus Democrat.

03:16:21.400 --> 03:16:26.466
And what that means
is the Democrat governors

03:16:26.466 --> 03:16:28.500
or the states with Democrat governors

03:16:28.500 --> 03:16:32.766
tend to have higher levels of openness
than the ones with Republican governors.

03:16:32.766 --> 03:16:35.200
That's
because have a negative coefficient there.

03:16:35.200 --> 03:16:38.600
But let's add a second block
and see how things shift around.

03:16:39.600 --> 03:16:44.800
Now, I do want to come up here and change
this to include the adjusted R squared.

03:16:44.800 --> 03:16:47.466
So I'm going to come down
and say model fit

03:16:48.333 --> 03:16:50.533
and add that on right there
and I'll close that.

03:16:52.200 --> 03:16:55.666
So now we have our model it simply knowing
whether a state has a Republican

03:16:55.666 --> 03:16:59.866
or Democrat governor predicts
almost 13% of the variance in the outcome.

03:17:00.300 --> 03:17:02.566
The openness of this state as a whole.

03:17:02.566 --> 03:17:04.866
And that's kind of remarkable on its own.

03:17:05.333 --> 03:17:08.000
But I'm going to come over here
and add a new block.

03:17:08.633 --> 03:17:12.033
And in block two,
we're going to try to put several other

03:17:12.033 --> 03:17:16.766
variables, say, for instance,
come down and pick just these last

03:17:16.800 --> 03:17:20.133
few museums, scrapbook and modern dance.

03:17:20.700 --> 03:17:22.533
And I'm going to put those into block to

03:17:24.500 --> 03:17:27.733
just as a main effect right there.

03:17:27.866 --> 03:17:32.433
And then I can compare the model
with just Governor

03:17:32.433 --> 03:17:35.500
and then the model with governor
and these three in it.

03:17:35.966 --> 03:17:37.400
And so that's what I have right here.

03:17:37.400 --> 03:17:41.566
We have this first one is just governor
and we have an adjusted R-squared of 12.9.

03:17:41.800 --> 03:17:44.900
It explains 13% of the variance
in the outcome openness.

03:17:45.333 --> 03:17:49.466
When we add these other three Google
search terms, the relative popularity

03:17:49.500 --> 03:17:53.033
of those on a state by state basis,
it goes up to 52%.

03:17:53.033 --> 03:17:56.666
We've now explained it
over 50% of the variance in the outcome,

03:17:57.000 --> 03:17:58.366
which is openness.

03:17:58.366 --> 03:18:02.600
Now that makes a little bit of sense
because openness often includes or

03:18:02.600 --> 03:18:05.900
creative openness, though it can include
a lot of other things as well.

03:18:06.633 --> 03:18:09.033
And this one right here, this

03:18:09.033 --> 03:18:13.000
model comparisons, tells us
that there is a statistically increase,

03:18:13.433 --> 03:18:15.700
which makes sense
because it's a huge jump.

03:18:15.700 --> 03:18:18.266
And then here we have the variables.

03:18:18.266 --> 03:18:19.366
Now, this is from model two.

03:18:19.366 --> 03:18:21.333
If we want to look at model one,

03:18:21.333 --> 03:18:23.933
we just on this
and we get to one with just governor.

03:18:23.933 --> 03:18:26.300
And you can see there's
a statistically significant effect there.

03:18:26.700 --> 03:18:29.433
That's the 0007 right here.

03:18:30.033 --> 03:18:32.366
But let's go back to model two

03:18:32.366 --> 03:18:35.833
and now we can see that gov is no longer
statistically significant.

03:18:35.833 --> 03:18:39.700
That's because it was a spurious
correlation as predicted by other things.

03:18:40.500 --> 03:18:43.133
And then music doesn't seem to matter.

03:18:43.333 --> 03:18:47.700
But we have strong and statistically
significant effects for both scrapbook

03:18:47.700 --> 03:18:53.333
and modern arts to creative interests,
and we see that one's and one's positive.

03:18:53.333 --> 03:18:58.933
So in the states that have higher levels
of interest in scrapbook as a search term,

03:18:59.233 --> 03:19:04.300
there are lower levels of openness
and in states that have higher interest

03:19:04.300 --> 03:19:07.633
in modern dance,
there are higher levels of openness.

03:19:08.033 --> 03:19:11.800
Again, that's because we have these
negative and positive coefficient ints,

03:19:12.400 --> 03:19:16.166
but remember, it's always
within the context of one another.

03:19:16.400 --> 03:19:20.133
Now if we want to,
we can add a third block where we take

03:19:20.133 --> 03:19:21.666
on, for instance, these other ones.

03:19:21.666 --> 03:19:26.266
We go from Instagram
and then I'll do a shift, click

03:19:26.400 --> 03:19:30.000
down to volunteering,
and I'll put those in the third

03:19:30.000 --> 03:19:32.866
block as main effects only.

03:19:33.133 --> 03:19:35.400
We're not looking for interactions
right now,

03:19:35.400 --> 03:19:39.433
and when I do that we can see that
adding those variables improved.

03:19:39.433 --> 03:19:43.733
The adjusted R-squared from only 0.5 to 5,

03:19:43.733 --> 03:19:47.633
that's 52 and a half percent to 57.2%.

03:19:47.633 --> 03:19:50.033
And in fact,
you can see here that that increase

03:19:50.400 --> 03:19:53.400
