three is not statistically significant.

03:19:53.400 --> 03:19:57.500
The increase
has a P value itself of 0.180.

03:19:57.666 --> 03:20:01.533
So we didn't get anything helpful
by adding those extra variables.

03:20:01.566 --> 03:20:04.200
In fact,
we lost degrees of freedom by doing that.

03:20:04.200 --> 03:20:06.166
So that tells us
that we're probably best off

03:20:06.166 --> 03:20:09.700
going with Model two,
which had a substantially higher

03:20:10.366 --> 03:20:14.233
rate of predictive ability
compared to the one I had just governor.

03:20:14.566 --> 03:20:18.700
And the results make sense and it's that
theoretical interpretation that matters.

03:20:19.066 --> 03:20:23.466
And so this is one of looking
at the relative contribution

03:20:23.800 --> 03:20:24.800
of different variables.

03:20:24.800 --> 03:20:28.066
Now, this one's theory driven
because you decide what the blocks are

03:20:28.366 --> 03:20:30.833
and you enter them in
and you interpret the results

03:20:31.133 --> 03:20:33.900
your own to see if they make sense
and how applicable they are.

03:20:34.200 --> 03:20:34.733
And really,

03:20:34.733 --> 03:20:35.433
whether it's something

03:20:35.433 --> 03:20:39.466
that you can put into use, whether you can
do something productive with it,

03:20:39.800 --> 03:20:43.800
that is the goal of linear regression
and this blocked entry

03:20:43.800 --> 03:20:46.600
is to find the information
that's going to be most useful to you

03:20:46.800 --> 03:20:49.300
in solving the problems
that you're dealing with

03:20:50.933 --> 03:20:53.800
before you
take the results of your linear regression

03:20:53.800 --> 03:20:57.000
and you go running off to market
and making massive changes,

03:20:57.300 --> 03:21:00.600
you do want to make sure
that you actually dotted

03:21:00.733 --> 03:21:04.800
I's and cross your TS
and you want to make sure that your data

03:21:04.966 --> 03:21:08.666
met the assumptions that the model has

03:21:08.666 --> 03:21:11.933
and that your data is leading
you in the right direction.

03:21:12.200 --> 03:21:16.100
This becomes a matter of checking
what are called regression diagnostics,

03:21:16.400 --> 03:21:20.733
a number of statistical
and graphical methods

03:21:21.000 --> 03:21:24.466
for looking at how
well your data fits the model.

03:21:24.733 --> 03:21:29.300
Now, I'm going to do this
by coming back to the same model and data

03:21:29.300 --> 03:21:33.066
that we used before where we're looking
at variables to predict the openness

03:21:33.066 --> 03:21:35.833
of people on a state by state basis.

03:21:36.266 --> 03:21:38.366
And I'm going to click on this model
right here

03:21:39.100 --> 03:21:43.800
and what I'm going to is I'm
going to come back not to model builder,

03:21:43.800 --> 03:21:47.666
although that is where I specified
the blocks that I was going to use.

03:21:48.433 --> 03:21:52.500
But I'm going to come down a little
to assumption checks

03:21:52.966 --> 03:21:55.800
and this is where the most important
things are going to happen.

03:21:56.400 --> 03:22:00.566
Now, there are a few here
that are particularly important

03:22:01.300 --> 03:22:05.400
if you have data that's measured
repeatedly over time,

03:22:05.400 --> 03:22:09.000
like quarter one, quarter to quarter
three results,

03:22:09.433 --> 03:22:13.000
then you're going to need to deal
with auto correlation to see

03:22:13.300 --> 03:22:17.033
how much a carryover effect
there is from one time to another.

03:22:17.033 --> 03:22:20.366
We don't have that right here,
so I'm not going to deal with that.

03:22:20.800 --> 03:22:25.200
But we do need to be worried
about coloniality or multicore linearity.

03:22:25.466 --> 03:22:29.166
And that's what happens when your
variables that you're using to predict

03:22:29.166 --> 03:22:31.766
the outcome are correlated
with each other.

03:22:32.333 --> 03:22:36.400
Now, really, the easiest way to check
that is to get a correlation coefficient

03:22:36.400 --> 03:22:40.733
and look at the associations
between your various variables.

03:22:41.133 --> 03:22:44.200
But the statistics
that are specific to linearity

03:22:44.200 --> 03:22:46.966
within linear regression
also tell you important things.

03:22:47.433 --> 03:22:48.733
We come down here, it's

03:22:48.733 --> 03:22:52.533
giving us two measures in particular,
and we're looking at model two.

03:22:52.533 --> 03:22:54.200
That's the one I have selected.

03:22:54.200 --> 03:22:56.966
The first is VHF,
which stands for the variance

03:22:56.966 --> 03:23:00.533
inflation factor
and the second one is tolerance.

03:23:00.533 --> 03:23:03.800
And there's a correlation
or an association between those.

03:23:04.033 --> 03:23:08.700
And what they're both referring to
in different ways is the association

03:23:08.933 --> 03:23:13.333
of each of these variables
with the other predictors in the model.

03:23:13.666 --> 03:23:17.466
And I can tell by looking at this that
we do have some crossover between them.

03:23:17.933 --> 03:23:21.300
The actual ways that they operate
gets a little complicated,

03:23:21.300 --> 03:23:22.900
and that's beyond what I'm trying to do
here.

03:23:22.900 --> 03:23:26.333
Mostly, I want you to know
that Jamari is able to do this for you

03:23:26.333 --> 03:23:31.700
and you can interpret the results in ways
that to make your model more robust,

03:23:33.166 --> 03:23:34.600
you can also do the Q.

03:23:34.600 --> 03:23:35.933
Q part of residuals.

03:23:35.933 --> 03:23:40.200
I have demonstrated that elsewhere,
and it's here to the residuals

03:23:40.200 --> 03:23:43.333
or the leftovers from predictions
based on your model

03:23:43.766 --> 03:23:47.966
need to be approximately
normally distributed.

03:23:47.966 --> 03:23:51.866
They need to not flare out
on one end of the model or the other.

03:23:52.100 --> 03:23:55.366
Now, I do see that there's one down here
dipping kind of low.

03:23:55.666 --> 03:23:58.833
The rest are basically on the line
and we come off a little bit.

03:23:59.366 --> 03:24:01.433
It's not horrible, though.

03:24:01.433 --> 03:24:03.633
You may want to do a little more
of a drill down

03:24:03.633 --> 03:24:06.566
analysis to see what's happening
in this particular dataset.

03:24:06.900 --> 03:24:08.466
There two ways you can do that.

03:24:08.466 --> 03:24:12.400
One is with the residual plot,
and that's going to do separate plots

03:24:12.400 --> 03:24:15.300
for each of the predictor variables
that we have in the model.

03:24:16.200 --> 03:24:18.533
And so it's going to a moment
to catch up with all of those.

03:24:18.933 --> 03:24:21.800
And the other one is
what's called cooks distance.

03:24:21.800 --> 03:24:26.933
And that's going to give you a measure
of the influence of specific cases.

03:24:26.933 --> 03:24:30.533
Now, because we have only 48 cases
in our data,

03:24:31.033 --> 03:24:35.466
it's the 48 continental United States
that's not such a horrible idea.

03:24:35.700 --> 03:24:38.333
And here we have
the plots that we're looking at.

03:24:38.666 --> 03:24:41.766
We see, for instance, with openness,
there's this pattern

03:24:41.766 --> 03:24:45.033
in the residuals
that we don't have in other things.

03:24:45.366 --> 03:24:47.966
And so that's issue
we may be concerned about.

03:24:47.966 --> 03:24:51.566
I'll click on Cook's Distance as well,
and Cook's Distance actually shows up

03:24:51.566 --> 03:24:57.000
above the charts that we have right here.

03:24:57.000 --> 03:24:58.700
And it's simply giving us the mean,

03:24:58.700 --> 03:25:01.433
the median standard deviation
and the range of these.

03:25:01.600 --> 03:25:04.300
And so you can look
at the individual scores that you have

03:25:04.300 --> 03:25:06.966
and try to find out
what's on in the residuals plots

03:25:07.300 --> 03:25:09.833
to see if there's something
that is deviating really sharply

03:25:09.833 --> 03:25:13.433
from your expectations or the assumptions
of the modeling technique.

03:25:14.500 --> 03:25:15.133
I do want

03:25:15.133 --> 03:25:19.100
finish with one other thing,
and that is the estimated marginal means.

03:25:19.566 --> 03:25:23.066
Now this will be more helpful
in some situations than in others,

03:25:23.333 --> 03:25:26.400
but what you can do here
is, you can actually try to get a chart

03:25:26.400 --> 03:25:30.600
that shows you how these variables
predict your outcome.

03:25:30.600 --> 03:25:32.600
So I can take governor,
which is an easy one

03:25:32.600 --> 03:25:37.333
because there's only two categories there
