03:25:37.833 --> 03:25:40.300
It'll be down here at the bottom.

03:25:40.300 --> 03:25:44.000
This simply gives me
the mean level of openness

03:25:44.200 --> 03:25:48.933
for the states with Democrat governors
and the mean level of openness for states

03:25:48.933 --> 03:25:50.000
with Republican governors.

03:25:50.000 --> 03:25:52.366
And you can actually see, well,
the means are a little different.

03:25:52.366 --> 03:25:54.866
This dot is a little lower than.

03:25:54.866 --> 03:25:58.333
This the confidence intervals overlap
substantially.

03:25:58.933 --> 03:26:02.200
And you can do this with other terms
if you want.

03:26:02.200 --> 03:26:04.266
We can take, for
instance, modern dance here

03:26:05.233 --> 03:26:06.700
and we can put that in.

03:26:06.700 --> 03:26:11.033
And it's going to be a little different
because this is a quantitative

03:26:11.033 --> 03:26:16.200
or continuous predictor, whereas
the governor was a nominal variable.

03:26:17.400 --> 03:26:20.366
And we'll scroll down a little bit
and what it is

03:26:20.400 --> 03:26:23.300
is kind of like a scatterplot,
but there's no data points on here.

03:26:23.666 --> 03:26:28.133
And Instead, we're looking at a regression
line with a confidence interval

03:26:28.133 --> 03:26:31.200
around it at the association
between the two

03:26:31.533 --> 03:26:34.166
as how modern dance predict

03:26:35.033 --> 03:26:40.333
the expected levels of openness
and you see as the relative interest

03:26:40.333 --> 03:26:45.000
in modern dance
goes up to about a level of four point

03:26:45.000 --> 03:26:49.866
something that the average level
of openness for the state goes up as well.

03:26:50.100 --> 03:26:52.900
And so this can be a way of
also seeing how well

03:26:53.100 --> 03:26:56.500
your data, the assumptions and fit in

03:26:56.766 --> 03:27:01.333
with the approach of linear regression,
which after all is one of the most common

03:27:01.333 --> 03:27:06.100
and most powerful methods
of using data to model specific outcomes.

03:27:06.100 --> 03:27:10.266
And something that Jamovi makes
incredibly easy to do and to interpret.

03:27:11.400 --> 03:27:13.466
One really common task in analyzing

03:27:13.500 --> 03:27:18.233
data is cases into one category or another

03:27:18.733 --> 03:27:21.600
based on a number of other variables
you might have.

03:27:21.600 --> 03:27:22.966
So for instance, with

03:27:22.966 --> 03:27:26.433
your computer is trying to decide
whether a particular email is spam or not.

03:27:26.833 --> 03:27:30.000
We are trying to decide
whether a particular person is likely

03:27:30.000 --> 03:27:34.900
to buy your product or not,
and those are dichotomous classifications.

03:27:35.166 --> 03:27:38.033
And a common method for analyzing

03:27:38.166 --> 03:27:43.000
or predicting dichotomous outcomes
is to use what's called binomial,

03:27:43.200 --> 03:27:47.500
which means two names
binomial logistic regression.

03:27:47.833 --> 03:27:53.300
So it's a form of linear regression,
but it's adapted for placing cases

03:27:53.300 --> 03:27:57.100
into one group
or another based on the probabilities

03:27:57.100 --> 03:27:59.533
that are predicted
from your other variables.

03:27:59.533 --> 03:28:03.633
Now, this is really easiest
to simply show how it works.

03:28:03.633 --> 03:28:07.200
So I'm going to use the data
I have about state data.

03:28:07.666 --> 03:28:11.100
I've got a number of variables
about personality

03:28:11.100 --> 03:28:14.900
characteristics
on a statewide basis and search terms.

03:28:15.500 --> 03:28:18.966
But the dichotomous variable
that I have in here is whether the state's

03:28:19.000 --> 03:28:23.133
governor, their current governor
right now is Republican or Democrat.

03:28:23.800 --> 03:28:24.933
Now, let's start with

03:28:24.933 --> 03:28:28.533
just a little tiny bit of exploration
so we know what we're dealing with.

03:28:29.266 --> 03:28:31.766
I'm going to take governor,
put it over here

03:28:32.300 --> 03:28:35.300
and we're going to get a frequency table

03:28:35.733 --> 03:28:38.166
and we will also get a bar plot.

03:28:38.600 --> 03:28:43.266
And so at this exact moment of the lower
48 states in the United states,

03:28:43.800 --> 03:28:46.766
about two thirds of them
have Republican governors.

03:28:47.033 --> 03:28:51.000
And so we're going to see if we can use
some of the other data

03:28:51.000 --> 03:28:56.500
we have in this dataset to classify
states or predict

03:28:56.833 --> 03:29:00.533
which ones have Republican governors
and which ones have Democrat governors.

03:29:01.033 --> 03:29:03.966
And so the way we want to do this,
I'll just close

03:29:03.966 --> 03:29:08.533
that is to come to regression
and come down here to logistic regression.

03:29:08.533 --> 03:29:13.733
Two outcomes or a binomial again we're
binomial means to names or to categories.

03:29:14.566 --> 03:29:15.366
I'll click on that.

03:29:16.433 --> 03:29:18.833
The first thing we
need to do is put our dependent variable

03:29:18.866 --> 03:29:21.000
as the outcome variable thing
we're trying to predict.

03:29:21.000 --> 03:29:22.600
And that's going to be

03:29:23.200 --> 03:29:25.500
and I don't even have to tell it,
you know,

03:29:25.500 --> 03:29:27.633
this is what this means
is what that means,

03:29:27.633 --> 03:29:30.933
because I actually have it
written as words in the dataset

03:29:31.366 --> 03:29:32.466
and the nice thing is that Jamovi

03:29:32.466 --> 03:29:35.000
is smart enough to be able to tell
that those are categories

03:29:36.066 --> 03:29:38.100
and then we get to pick some covariates.

03:29:38.100 --> 03:29:39.633
Now, I could pick a lot here.

03:29:39.633 --> 03:29:44.066
Let's pick
just the social media ones, just for fun.

03:29:44.400 --> 03:29:49.366
So I'm going to come down here
to Instagram, Facebook and retweet.

03:29:49.633 --> 03:29:52.700
By the way, the reason says retweet
is because Google Correlate

03:29:52.700 --> 03:29:54.033
wouldn't let me search for Twitter.

03:29:54.033 --> 03:29:57.466
I don't know why, but since Retweet

03:29:57.466 --> 03:30:01.100
is exclusively a Twitter word,
it seemed like a good substitute.

03:30:01.100 --> 03:30:03.333
So I'm going to put those
all into covariates.

03:30:03.733 --> 03:30:09.300
And those are the three variables that I'm
going to use together to try to predict

03:30:09.300 --> 03:30:13.333
which states have Democrat governors,
in which states have Republican governors.

03:30:13.966 --> 03:30:17.733
And you can see right here, we've got a
model that's actually working pretty well.

03:30:18.166 --> 03:30:20.100
We've got three variables.

03:30:20.100 --> 03:30:22.400
We have The Intercept, which is not zero.

03:30:23.100 --> 03:30:26.000
Instagram is not statistically
significant.

03:30:26.033 --> 03:30:28.533
Facebook is in retweets.

03:30:28.533 --> 03:30:29.766
No, we're close.

03:30:29.766 --> 03:30:33.833
But there's a lot of other information
we can get through the options

03:30:33.833 --> 03:30:35.400
that we have in movie.

03:30:35.400 --> 03:30:36.666
So let's just take a quick look.

03:30:36.666 --> 03:30:40.733
Let's take a look at model builder
and this is how we want to enter things

03:30:40.733 --> 03:30:43.200
and we can put stuff in blocks
if we want to.

03:30:43.500 --> 03:30:44.000
I don't feel

03:30:44.000 --> 03:30:47.166
the need to do that in this case,
but I showed how that works in the video

03:30:47.166 --> 03:30:49.033
on linear regression.

03:30:49.033 --> 03:30:50.800
Let's look at reference levels

03:30:52.300 --> 03:30:55.133
and one
of the categories needs to be taken

03:30:55.133 --> 03:30:58.500
as the baseline and we're trying
to predict a change to the other category

03:30:59.100 --> 03:31:01.333
because there are more Republicans
governors.

03:31:01.333 --> 03:31:04.566
Let's just have Democrat as the baseline
and we'll go up from there.

03:31:05.166 --> 03:31:08.333
Assumption
checks colony parity isn't the issue,

03:31:08.333 --> 03:31:11.666
especially because I'm
pretty sure that these three social media

03:31:11.666 --> 03:31:14.300
terms, as Google search terms are related.

03:31:14.733 --> 03:31:17.466
And from that we get both the VHF, the

03:31:17.733 --> 03:31:20.833
the variance inflation factor
and the tolerance.

03:31:20.833 --> 03:31:24.200
And there's indications here
that we've got some colony ease,

03:31:25.000 --> 03:31:27.166
but nothing awful.

03:31:27.500 --> 03:31:30.566
We have a few choices
on how we assess fit.

03:31:30.566 --> 03:31:35.000
I'm going to leave this with the default
where it uses deviance in the AIC,

03:31:35.000 --> 03:31:37.633
which is the key information criterion.

03:31:38.400 --> 03:31:41.533
Then we go to the model coefficients.

03:31:41.533 --> 03:31:45.666
A common choice when you're dealing
with binomial logistic regression

03:31:45.666 --> 03:31:49.566
is the odds ratio, and it's also nice
to get a confidence interval

03:31:49.933 --> 03:31:53.700
and that's going to add
a few columns on to this table right here.

03:31:54.033 --> 03:31:56.100
You see we've got the odds ratio
right there now

03:31:56.500 --> 03:31:59.966
and so many other statistics, zero

03:32:00.066 --> 03:32:04.566
is the base value and things either go
positive or negative, but the nothing's

03:32:04.566 --> 03:32:09.066
happening value for an odds
ratio is one that means a 1 to 1 ratio

03:32:09.700 --> 03:32:13.466
and it goes below one,
but it doesn't go all the way down

03:32:13.466 --> 03:32:16.266
to zero, can't get all the way down there
and can go up from there.

03:32:16.666 --> 03:32:20.466
And so you see, for instance,
that the intercept well,

03:32:21.033 --> 03:32:25.000
the intercept is reliably above zero,
but you can see that it's a odds ratio.

03:32:25.033 --> 03:32:27.000
Both of these numbers are above one.

03:32:27.000 --> 03:32:30.166
This one one's below one, one's up these.

03:32:30.166 --> 03:32:31.700
Both of them are above one.

03:32:31.700 --> 03:32:33.300
They're on the same side.

03:32:33.300 --> 03:32:36.900
And so these give us an idea
of the variables

03:32:37.233 --> 03:32:40.266
that predict
the odds of a particular state.

03:32:40.266 --> 03:32:42.700
Having a Republican governor
based on these three

03:32:42.700 --> 03:32:46.466
social media variables
that we have from Google Correlate,

03:32:47.066 --> 03:32:51.533
if we come down a little further,
we can go to estimated marginal means.

03:32:52.200 --> 03:32:56.166
And so, for instance,
Facebook was significant

03:32:56.166 --> 03:32:59.666
within the context
of these three predictor variables.

03:32:59.666 --> 03:33:03.033
Let's take that and stick it into here
for marginal means

03:33:03.033 --> 03:33:06.433
and actually gets a nice curved chart
that goes with it.

03:33:07.100 --> 03:33:08.500
If I come down here for a moment

03:33:09.900 --> 03:33:10.533
and what this

03:33:10.533 --> 03:33:13.766
shows us is the probability of a state

03:33:13.800 --> 03:33:17.600
having a Republican governor
going from 0 to 1.

03:33:18.000 --> 03:33:21.333
That's 0% to 100% based on the Z score

03:33:21.366 --> 03:33:24.533
that State has on searches
for Facebook, on Google.

03:33:24.966 --> 03:33:29.166
And you can see here that when states
search less than other states

03:33:29.166 --> 03:33:32.833
for Facebook, they are less likely
to have a Republican governor.

03:33:33.300 --> 03:33:36.600
But when they search more, they're more
likely to have a Republican governor.

03:33:36.933 --> 03:33:42.200
And so this a nice way
of looking at the effect of that,

03:33:42.200 --> 03:33:46.066
because binary logistic regression
does work on a curve system where it's

03:33:46.166 --> 03:33:49.300
drawing this probably changing over time.

03:33:49.833 --> 03:33:51.966
This one shows you just the one variable.

03:33:51.966 --> 03:33:55.833
It actually uses all of the variables
together to calculate probabilities.

03:33:56.533 --> 03:33:59.633
But another
nice thing about categorization

03:33:59.633 --> 03:34:03.233
tasks like this is
you can get a classification table.

03:34:03.966 --> 03:34:06.233
So I can click on that one right here.

03:34:06.233 --> 03:34:09.600
And what is going to tell me
is what it predicts

03:34:09.700 --> 03:34:12.233
the states will have versus
what they actually do.

03:34:12.600 --> 03:34:15.566
And what's interesting too
is you can change the cutoff value.

03:34:16.200 --> 03:34:19.366
So for right now, it's
only getting 40% of the Democrat states.

03:34:19.366 --> 03:34:22.566
Correct, it's
getting 91% of the Republican ones.

03:34:23.000 --> 03:34:25.766
But let's take a quick
look at what's called a cutoff plot,

03:34:25.766 --> 03:34:30.333
which gives us a chart of what's called
specificity and sensitivity.

03:34:30.600 --> 03:34:32.966
You can think of sensitivity, meaning like

03:34:33.600 --> 03:34:37.266
it's very likely to set off an alarm

03:34:37.266 --> 03:34:40.700
or to give you the answer if it believes
it has a Republican governor.

03:34:41.300 --> 03:34:44.666
And specificity means it's
going to do that only if it does.

03:34:45.133 --> 03:34:48.866
And in many situations these two lines,

03:34:48.866 --> 03:34:52.800
this is specificity going up here
and this is sensitivity going down.

03:34:53.266 --> 03:34:56.033
Often
they cross right here at the 50% point.

03:34:56.333 --> 03:34:58.566
But these ones are a lot closer to 0.7.

03:34:58.566 --> 03:35:01.800
So what I'm actually going to do
is I'm going to change the cutoff

03:35:01.800 --> 03:35:05.266
from point five, 2.7,
and do that right here.

03:35:06.200 --> 03:35:10.133
And you'll see that it changes
the way the classification table works,

03:35:10.500 --> 03:35:13.433
because now it's going to say,
well, only put them as Republican

03:35:13.433 --> 03:35:17.300
if they have over a 70% chance
of being Republican.

03:35:17.300 --> 03:35:21.200
And that actually makes sense to
because it's about two thirds Republican

03:35:21.200 --> 03:35:24.600
governors
slightly over in the country as a whole.

03:35:25.400 --> 03:35:28.866
You see this cutoff lines
lot closer to where these two crossovers

03:35:29.066 --> 03:35:32.666
usually where you want to get it
because it's going to be maximum utility

03:35:32.666 --> 03:35:37.333
there and it's changed
the classification table instead of 40%.

03:35:37.333 --> 03:35:40.700
We now have 73% correct
for the Democratic governors

03:35:41.133 --> 03:35:44.200
and we've gone from 91 to 73.

03:35:44.200 --> 03:35:46.300
But now it's sort of balanced

03:35:46.500 --> 03:35:49.566
in terms of how accurate it is
for the two different conditions.

03:35:49.966 --> 03:35:52.433
And so these are some of the methods
that Jamovi gives you

03:35:52.433 --> 03:35:55.600
for looking at the relationship
between several predictor variables,

03:35:55.600 --> 03:35:58.066
like the three social media search terms

03:35:58.800 --> 03:36:01.933
and how they can be used to predict
the classification

03:36:01.933 --> 03:36:07.000
of a dichotomous outcome like Republican
or Democrat Governor or any other time,

03:36:07.233 --> 03:36:10.466
you have two distinct outcomes
that you're trying to predict.

03:36:11.700 --> 03:36:15.000
We're at a point where things are going
to get very complicated.

03:36:15.000 --> 03:36:17.100
At least they could very complicated.

03:36:17.533 --> 03:36:20.033
Multan Normal logistic regression

03:36:20.533 --> 03:36:24.033
is potentially
a very sophisticated analysis.

03:36:24.266 --> 03:36:28.500
What you're trying to do is several
predictor variables in a regression

03:36:28.500 --> 03:36:33.333
equation to predict not two categories,
but several categories.

03:36:33.733 --> 03:36:36.366
And although it may not sound
like a big change,

03:36:36.400 --> 03:36:40.200
the processing behind
it becomes exponentially more complicated.

03:36:40.566 --> 03:36:45.566
Fortunately, Jamovi makes it possible
to do a relatively simple, multichannel

03:36:45.733 --> 03:36:47.633
logistic regression.

03:36:47.633 --> 03:36:50.433
I'm going to demonstrate this
with the state data,

03:36:50.900 --> 03:36:52.066
and I want to look at this one

03:36:52.066 --> 03:36:55.766
category here, which actually has to do
with psychology profile

03:36:55.900 --> 03:36:58.333
for the various states
in the United States.

03:36:58.866 --> 03:37:00.000
Let's start by doing this.

03:37:00.000 --> 03:37:03.300
Let's do a quick exploration
and get descriptive

03:37:05.366 --> 03:37:06.700
for psych regions.

03:37:06.700 --> 03:37:11.033
I'm going to put that over here
and I want to get the frequency table

03:37:11.033 --> 03:37:13.433
and I want to get the bar plot

03:37:14.033 --> 03:37:17.400
and this is based on an analysis

03:37:17.400 --> 03:37:21.066
of people's online behavior
and other characteristics.

03:37:21.466 --> 03:37:26.300
And the researchers ended up with three
category is what they called a friendly

03:37:26.300 --> 03:37:32.133
and conventional, which is half the states
in the sample, relaxed and creative.

03:37:32.500 --> 03:37:34.666
That's ten states who are about 21

03:37:35.500 --> 03:37:38.000
and temperamental and uninhibited.

03:37:38.000 --> 03:37:41.033
That's 14 of the states in their sample.

03:37:41.466 --> 03:37:43.400
And here's a bar chart. You can see them.

03:37:43.400 --> 03:37:46.600
And unfortunately, labels
don't automatically adjust

03:37:46.600 --> 03:37:49.166
in a movie at this point,
but that'll happen eventually.

03:37:50.100 --> 03:37:53.633
But let's see how we can use
other variables to predict

03:37:54.300 --> 03:37:56.400
which states into which categories.

03:37:56.833 --> 03:37:59.733
And what I'm going to do for
that is I'm going to come back up here

03:38:00.300 --> 03:38:03.000
to regression and go to an outcome

03:38:03.000 --> 03:38:05.400
that's multi normal logistic regression.

03:38:06.000 --> 03:38:09.600
And when I click on that,
the first thing I have to do is

03:38:09.600 --> 03:38:13.166
pick what my dependent variable is, what's
the thing that I'm trying to predict?

03:38:13.500 --> 03:38:16.233
In this case, it's the regions.

03:38:16.233 --> 03:38:17.933
So I'm going to put that right there.

03:38:17.933 --> 03:38:20.433
Now I've chosen one
with just three categories.

03:38:20.433 --> 03:38:21.566
That's going to be the simplest.

03:38:22.500 --> 03:38:24.900
If we had only two, we do binomial

03:38:25.300 --> 03:38:29.033
and if we had more than that,
it just gets so complicated.

03:38:29.033 --> 03:38:32.033
It's hard to deal with
in terms of covariates.

03:38:32.033 --> 03:38:36.066
What I'm going to do is I'm going to pick
the five elements of the big

03:38:36.066 --> 03:38:38.300
five personality factors.

03:38:38.300 --> 03:38:41.666
So that's extraversion, agreeableness,
conscientiousness, neuroticism

03:38:41.666 --> 03:38:42.666
and openness.

03:38:42.666 --> 03:38:45.800
Mind you,
this is not a nice person by person level.

03:38:45.800 --> 03:38:47.866
It's a state by state level.

03:38:48.300 --> 03:38:50.433
I'm going to slide
that over into covariates

03:38:51.766 --> 03:38:55.600
and then from there we can start
looking at the model that it creates,

03:38:56.700 --> 03:38:59.433
it takes a moment
sometimes for it to run through this

03:38:59.433 --> 03:39:03.200
because there's a lot of calculating
that goes on behind it.

03:39:03.200 --> 03:39:08.433
Let me close this
so I can slide this over for a second.

03:39:08.433 --> 03:39:09.666
We have a pretty big table here.

03:39:09.666 --> 03:39:12.933
The first one is the fit measures.

03:39:12.933 --> 03:39:17.666
So we have deviance, we have the AIC,
and you'll see that a lot of

03:39:17.666 --> 03:39:21.300
this is very similar to what we had
in the binomial logistic regression.

03:39:21.733 --> 03:39:26.766
We also have the model coefficient
where it's comparing the different groups,

03:39:26.766 --> 03:39:30.433
the relaxin creative minus
the friendly and conventional.

03:39:30.900 --> 03:39:35.333
And it's looking at these various
elements, the personality factors,

03:39:35.766 --> 03:39:38.166
and we have the P values over here
and we see that, for instance,

03:39:38.166 --> 03:39:40.266
when distinguishing between
relax and creative,

03:39:40.600 --> 03:39:42.866
none of these seem
to make a big difference.

03:39:43.433 --> 03:39:45.933
When we look at temperamental
and uninhibited

03:39:45.933 --> 03:39:49.133
versus friendly, conventional
do get one and two conscientiousness

03:39:49.133 --> 03:39:53.233
is appears to be an important one
and neuroticism is nearly there.

03:39:54.433 --> 03:39:57.800
Now, let's take
a look at some of the options we have.

03:39:57.800 --> 03:40:00.133
Click back on this

03:40:00.133 --> 03:40:01.700
and then come down to Model Builder.

03:40:01.700 --> 03:40:04.266
I can put the variables in in blocks
if I want.

03:40:04.266 --> 03:40:06.533
And there may be situations where since

03:40:07.366 --> 03:40:09.266
and there may be situations
in which I want to do that.

03:40:09.266 --> 03:40:12.700
But with just these five personality ones,
I'm not going to do blocks.

03:40:13.200 --> 03:40:15.633
But I did demonstrate that in linear
regression.

03:40:15.633 --> 03:40:16.533
So you could take a

03:40:16.533 --> 03:40:20.433
at that reference level is what's the one
I want to compare it all to.

03:40:20.433 --> 03:40:24.533
I actually think I'm going
to take the temperamental and uninhibited

03:40:24.533 --> 03:40:26.400
and make that the reference level

03:40:26.400 --> 03:40:29.100
and you can see how the order
just switches around over here.

03:40:29.366 --> 03:40:33.366
So this is friendly, conventional,
minus temperamental and uninhibited,

03:40:34.066 --> 03:40:38.100
and it just changes the sign
of some of the comparisons.

03:40:38.700 --> 03:40:42.133
Although you see down here,
neuroticism becomes an extremely big thing

03:40:42.433 --> 03:40:43.366
for distinguishing

03:40:43.366 --> 03:40:46.600
between the relaxed and creative
and the temperamental and uninhibited.

03:40:47.533 --> 03:40:49.133
We're going to follow up with that.

03:40:50.300 --> 03:40:52.200
We'll come down to model fit.

03:40:52.200 --> 03:40:54.200
These are the standard choices here.

03:40:54.200 --> 03:40:57.533
We'll keep those as they are
and model coefficients.

03:40:57.966 --> 03:41:00.933
Now this is where we have the estimates.

03:41:00.933 --> 03:41:04.233
Those are coefficients,
but it's nice to have odds ratios

03:41:04.233 --> 03:41:06.500
and confidence intervals those as well.

03:41:07.500 --> 03:41:12.166
And when I do that,
I just get a few more columns over here.

03:41:12.766 --> 03:41:14.900
Now you can ignore this one
because it's for the intercept.

03:41:14.900 --> 03:41:17.066
We don't expect anything particular there.

03:41:17.066 --> 03:41:19.466
It's the rest of the ones
that we're interested in.

03:41:19.466 --> 03:41:22.300
And you can see, for instance, here this

03:41:22.300 --> 03:41:24.400
second, the bottom one, this is

03:41:24.400 --> 03:41:27.166
relaxing, creative,
minus temperamental and uninhibited.

03:41:27.500 --> 03:41:30.666
We're looking at neuroticism,
which has to do with

03:41:31.800 --> 03:41:35.700
kind of having a lot of mood swings
or also getting irritable.

03:41:37.000 --> 03:41:41.833
And we have a significant association
here.

03:41:42.266 --> 03:41:44.366
The odds ratio, 0.73.

03:41:44.566 --> 03:41:49.133
Remember the null value for north
ratio is one.

03:41:49.300 --> 03:41:52.800
If it's either below one or above one,
then you have something going on.

03:41:53.166 --> 03:41:56.966
And especially if both sides of the
confidence interval are on the same side

03:41:57.000 --> 03:41:58.433
of one as we see here.

03:41:59.900 --> 03:42:00.933
But this is all going to be

03:42:00.933 --> 03:42:03.966
a lot easier to interpret
if we get the graphs.

03:42:03.966 --> 03:42:07.366
And so I'm going to come down here
to the estimated marginal means,

03:42:07.800 --> 03:42:09.233
and that's going to take a moment
because I'm

03:42:09.233 --> 03:42:12.000
going to put all of them in here
and I have to do them one at a time.

