03:53:30.266 --> 03:53:33.900
frequency is in January,
we get a new way to deal with data.

03:53:34.133 --> 03:53:38.700
Specifically, we are now dealing
with counting as opposed to measuring.

03:53:38.800 --> 03:53:40.166
Now measuring means when you say

03:53:40.166 --> 03:53:43.700
how long does it take to do something
or What's the average score here?

03:53:44.033 --> 03:53:46.766
Counting
is like counting the number of rocks where

03:53:46.933 --> 03:53:51.666
able to enumerate the cases
and put the frequencies in as your data.

03:53:51.666 --> 03:53:53.933
And this requires a different approach.

03:53:53.933 --> 03:53:57.466
In GMAT, we have several options
for analyzing frequencies.

03:53:57.866 --> 03:54:00.333
The first and simplest is
what's called the binomial test.

03:54:00.333 --> 03:54:04.666
Binomial means two names, and it's fair
when you have two categories of outcomes,

03:54:04.866 --> 03:54:08.266
like flipping a coin and getting the
of heads and the number of tails.

03:54:09.333 --> 03:54:11.566
If you have more than two outcomes,
say for instance,

03:54:11.566 --> 03:54:14.466
you have baskets of several
different kinds of mushrooms.

03:54:14.766 --> 03:54:17.866
You can use the chi squared
goodness of fit test to see

03:54:18.100 --> 03:54:21.933
how things are distributed
across those multiple categories.

03:54:22.900 --> 03:54:24.266
If you want to look at two

03:54:24.266 --> 03:54:26.400
categories at once
and look at the association

03:54:26.400 --> 03:54:28.100
and say, for instance,
you want to look at something

03:54:28.100 --> 03:54:31.833
like the number of World
Cup teams from different countries

03:54:31.833 --> 03:54:35.700
and break it down by male or female,
you know, because know

03:54:35.700 --> 03:54:38.733
that in soccer the American women
have done fabulously well.

03:54:38.733 --> 03:54:40.800
The American men
didn't qualify for the World Cup.

03:54:40.800 --> 03:54:45.233
Most recently, that's looking
at an association between two categories.

03:54:45.233 --> 03:54:49.200
And you can analyze that with what's
called the Chi Square test of association.

03:54:50.233 --> 03:54:52.333
You also have something
called a McNamara's test.

03:54:52.333 --> 03:54:54.300
This is a relatively unusual one.

03:54:54.300 --> 03:54:58.200
It's for looking at tables
where you have related data, paired

03:54:58.200 --> 03:55:01.766
observations like data from twins,
because they go together

03:55:02.000 --> 03:55:05.066
and you have to take together
that consideration.

03:55:05.066 --> 03:55:08.166
And also, if you did the same person
at time one and time two,

03:55:08.466 --> 03:55:09.933
then the frequencies

03:55:09.933 --> 03:55:12.800
have this built in association
and you need to compensate for that.

03:55:12.800 --> 03:55:15.900
McNamara's test lets you do that.

03:55:15.900 --> 03:55:19.233
And then finally there's also log linear
regression.

03:55:19.233 --> 03:55:22.300
It accomplishes a lot of the same things,
but it uses a different

03:55:22.300 --> 03:55:24.600
mathematical approach.
It's based on regression.

03:55:24.600 --> 03:55:26.866
There are some pretty serious equations
that go into it,

03:55:27.166 --> 03:55:30.700
but it is often a very flexible
and very powerful technique

03:55:30.700 --> 03:55:33.733
for modeling the number of cases,

03:55:33.733 --> 03:55:37.433
the observation genes are frequencies
within different cells of your data.

03:55:38.533 --> 03:55:38.833
And so

03:55:38.833 --> 03:55:41.866
taken together,
this gives us a great range of options

03:55:42.066 --> 03:55:45.600
for a data analysis,
for counting infrequent CS In Germany,

