03:57:04.133 --> 03:57:06.866
And we have 15 Democrat governors

03:57:06.900 --> 03:57:11.400
as of about a week ago and 33 Republicans.

03:57:11.400 --> 03:57:13.633
And here's a bar chart
that shows the difference.

03:57:13.633 --> 03:57:15.100
And so that's really easy to see.

03:57:15.100 --> 03:57:16.966
It's about 2 to 1.

03:57:16.966 --> 03:57:20.666
Now in terms of binomial test,
what we're doing is trying to tell

03:57:20.700 --> 03:57:24.166
whether our observed proportions differ

03:57:24.900 --> 03:57:27.366
significantly from some value.

03:57:27.700 --> 03:57:31.900
In a lot of inferential tests, it's really
obvious that the null value is zero.

03:57:32.433 --> 03:57:35.133
Well, in the binomial test
you have a few choices.

03:57:35.600 --> 03:57:38.166
Now let me close this
and come over to frequencies.

03:57:38.166 --> 03:57:42.233
And the first thing we're going to look at
is the two outcomes or binomial test.

03:57:42.233 --> 03:57:44.666
It's under one sample proportion tests.

03:57:44.666 --> 03:57:48.233
If I click on that, it's
actually a really simple dialog box.

03:57:48.233 --> 03:57:50.600
All I need to do is pick the variable
that I want.

03:57:50.600 --> 03:57:54.300
I'm going to use gov
and move it over into this box.

03:57:54.300 --> 03:57:57.766
You can tell it's looking for
that is coded in g movie

03:57:57.766 --> 03:58:00.933
as either a nominal
that is a categorical variable or ordinal.

03:58:01.800 --> 03:58:04.200
And right now it's already done the test

03:58:04.200 --> 03:58:07.566
and it has me that these proportions

03:58:07.566 --> 03:58:12.466
are both significantly different
from a null value of 50%.

03:58:13.500 --> 03:58:15.866
And I can get a confidence interval

03:58:15.866 --> 03:58:18.600
which actually is a really nice idea
in this particular case.

03:58:19.200 --> 03:58:23.366
And this lets me know
that the percentage of Democrat governors

03:58:23.400 --> 03:58:25.833
has a 95% confidence that goes from

03:58:26.866 --> 03:58:29.733
18.7% to 46.3%.

03:58:29.733 --> 03:58:33.066
And we have the flip side of that
for the Republican governors now.

03:58:33.066 --> 03:58:37.266
I'm going to close this for just second
so I can open it up again.

03:58:37.266 --> 03:58:41.633
This one is done with a null value of 0.5
or 50% each.

03:58:42.000 --> 03:58:45.000
And maybe that's not the value
that we want to use.

03:58:45.000 --> 03:58:48.900
You know, right now
we have 68.8% Republican governors.

03:58:49.166 --> 03:58:51.300
Let's say we want to compare it
to maybe ten years ago.

03:58:51.300 --> 03:58:53.866
And I don't know what the proportion of

03:58:54.366 --> 03:58:58.500
and Democrat governors in the 48
contiguous states was ten years ago.

03:58:58.800 --> 03:59:02.600
But let's just say, for instance, I'm
going to do the confidence interval here.

03:59:03.033 --> 03:59:07.066
Let's say it was 60, and we want to know
whether there's been a change

03:59:07.066 --> 03:59:08.666
in the last ten years.

03:59:08.666 --> 03:59:10.333
Then I can do this.

03:59:10.333 --> 03:59:14.166
And now it's going to compare
these two numbers to 60%.

03:59:14.166 --> 03:59:17.300
And what we see here
is it's a little different.

03:59:17.300 --> 03:59:19.300
Let me close this so we can see this

03:59:20.700 --> 03:59:24.000
in the first case
because we're comparing to this 50% and.

03:59:24.000 --> 03:59:26.766
Each of these two values,
because they have to add up to

03:59:27.600 --> 03:59:30.633
100% are equidistant from 50%.

03:59:30.900 --> 03:59:33.900
The p value is the same
for the both of them on.

03:59:33.900 --> 03:59:38.000
The other hand, when we move the null
value away from 50%, in this case

03:59:38.000 --> 03:59:42.200
2.6 or 60%,
then the P values are very different.

03:59:42.200 --> 03:59:46.966
We find that the current observed
proportion of Republican governors,

03:59:46.966 --> 03:59:50.400
which is 0.688,
which is the same as 68.8%,

03:59:50.733 --> 03:59:54.733
that does not differ
significantly from 60%.

03:59:55.066 --> 03:59:57.933
On the other hand,
the number of Democrats does.

03:59:58.233 --> 04:00:01.066
And so we have this big difference

04:00:01.233 --> 04:00:06.100
depending on how you set the null value
and is going to depend what value you is.

04:00:06.100 --> 04:00:08.966
If it's something that really should be
just as likely yes is no,

04:00:09.033 --> 04:00:11.333
then the default value of point
five is good.

04:00:11.333 --> 04:00:12.266
But if you're looking, for instance,

04:00:12.266 --> 04:00:15.600
at a game that has four players,
then the default value of any one player

04:00:15.600 --> 04:00:19.500
when you point to five
and there can be other values

04:00:19.500 --> 04:00:23.333
depending on what you think
is most likely to happen at.

04:00:23.333 --> 04:00:27.033
But no matter how you do it,
the proportion test is a very easy test

04:00:27.033 --> 04:00:31.000
to do in movie and it gives you
the simplest kind of data.

04:00:31.000 --> 04:00:33.066
Just a simple yes no outcome.

04:00:33.066 --> 04:00:37.066
And it does both an inferential test
with a probability value

04:00:37.066 --> 04:00:38.266
and a confidence interval.

04:00:39.466 --> 04:00:42.600
When you're
looking at the number of cases,

04:00:42.600 --> 04:00:46.766
either of two categories,
you can do the binomial test,

04:00:46.766 --> 04:00:49.233
a very simple test,
and we've show that elsewhere.

04:00:49.233 --> 04:00:51.033
But when you have more than two

04:00:51.033 --> 04:00:54.900
categories, but still in a single variable
or a factor,

04:00:55.266 --> 04:00:59.000
then you get to do the Chi square
two goodness of fit test.

04:00:59.000 --> 04:01:03.400
And it's kind of a silly long name,
but it's test you use for comparing

04:01:03.533 --> 04:01:06.300
multiple categories in the same variable.

04:01:06.533 --> 04:01:09.566
Let me give you an easy demonstration
of this using the state

04:01:09.566 --> 04:01:12.266
data that you can download
from DataLab Dot CC.

04:01:13.433 --> 04:01:17.633
In this example, I'm going to use this
variable that's called psych regions.

04:01:17.633 --> 04:01:21.066
It's based on psychological research
that builds profiles

04:01:21.266 --> 04:01:23.633
states in the United States.

04:01:23.633 --> 04:01:24.633
And we're going to start

04:01:24.633 --> 04:01:27.800
with an exploration
where we get basic descriptive of this.

04:01:28.833 --> 04:01:31.200
All I'm going to do is pick psych
regions, move,

04:01:31.200 --> 04:01:35.466
whatever the variables,
and I'm going to change the statistics.

04:01:35.466 --> 04:01:36.500
We don't need these ones

04:01:36.500 --> 04:01:41.166
because those are for quantitative
or continuous variables.

04:01:41.166 --> 04:01:42.133
There we go.

04:01:42.733 --> 04:01:45.566
I'm going to get the frequency table

04:01:45.566 --> 04:01:48.166
and I'm going to get a bar plot.

04:01:48.300 --> 04:01:50.933
It's really simple stuff.

04:01:50.933 --> 04:01:54.200
Now, of the 48 contiguous states

04:01:54.200 --> 04:01:57.433
that the researchers looked at,
they classified 24 of them.

04:01:57.433 --> 04:01:59.233
That's exactly half as friendly.

04:01:59.233 --> 04:02:00.900
And convention.

04:02:00.900 --> 04:02:02.666
That's our biggest bar right here.

04:02:02.666 --> 04:02:04.833
I'm sorry that the labels are overlapping.

04:02:04.833 --> 04:02:07.200
I'm sure that will be fixed
in a later release of movie.

04:02:08.400 --> 04:02:13.300
The next category is relaxed and Creative,
which includes ten of the 48 states.

04:02:13.300 --> 04:02:15.366
That's about 21%.

04:02:15.366 --> 04:02:18.800
And the last category is temperamental
and uninhibited.

04:02:18.800 --> 04:02:22.133
That's 14 of the 48 states, or about 29%.

04:02:22.966 --> 04:02:25.833
And so we can look and see, for example,

04:02:25.833 --> 04:02:29.366
whether the states are evenly distributed
across these three categories.

04:02:29.366 --> 04:02:33.066
Now can tell by looking at the bars
that obviously they're not all the same.

04:02:33.300 --> 04:02:36.966
The question is whether that difference
is statistically significant,

04:02:37.233 --> 04:02:40.666
and that's where we do
the Chi Square two goodness of fit test.

04:02:41.200 --> 04:02:44.066
Now there's an important choice
we get to make, and that's

04:02:44.066 --> 04:02:48.433
how we define our null values by default
is going to assume

04:02:48.433 --> 04:02:52.533
equal frequencies
or equal proportions in each category.

04:02:52.866 --> 04:02:56.266
So let's go to the frequencies
here and go to any outcomes.

04:02:56.300 --> 04:02:57.933
That means more than two outcomes,

04:02:57.933 --> 04:03:01.766
an arbitrary number
and that right there is chi squared.

04:03:02.066 --> 04:03:03.100
It looks like an ax.

04:03:03.100 --> 04:03:05.700
It's actually a capital Greek c,

04:03:06.666 --> 04:03:08.400
it's called chi chi squared.

04:03:08.400 --> 04:03:10.500
Goodness of fit.
So we're going to click on that one.

04:03:11.033 --> 04:03:13.600
And all we need to do
is take our variable here

04:03:13.600 --> 04:03:16.733
as psych regions
and put it right here and the variables.

04:03:17.100 --> 04:03:21.933
Now this option for counts
is when you have a summary table

04:03:21.933 --> 04:03:25.600
in your data set and I'll show you that
in a later video how that can work.

04:03:26.033 --> 04:03:28.600
But because we have a raw data where,

04:03:28.600 --> 04:03:31.800
everything is listed as one uppercase,
which is most common.

04:03:32.100 --> 04:03:34.233
That's the kind
we're going to use right here.

04:03:34.233 --> 04:03:38.733
And what we have over here
is a proportions test where it says that

04:03:39.800 --> 04:03:45.066
a proportion of 0.5,
that 50% are in this 128 to 9 two.

04:03:45.466 --> 04:03:49.766
And it tells us that the chi squared
goodness if, it gives an inferential value

04:03:50.100 --> 04:03:53.300
where the calculated value of chi squared
is 6.5,

04:03:53.700 --> 04:03:56.666
two degrees of freedom
gives us a p value of .039.

04:03:57.133 --> 04:04:02.133
The standard of P or probability that
we use for significance testing is 0.05.

04:04:02.133 --> 04:04:04.600
So this would be
a statistically significant finding.

04:04:05.133 --> 04:04:08.200
But I want to show you two
other things we can do.

04:04:08.200 --> 04:04:13.066
One is exactly what is the algorithm
comparing these values against?

04:04:13.066 --> 04:04:16.033
Well,
is comparing them against expected counts.

04:04:16.500 --> 04:04:19.866
And the way it does expected counts,
is it right now?

04:04:19.866 --> 04:04:23.266
It just takes however many values we have
and it splits them evenly across

04:04:23.266 --> 04:04:24.600
a number of categories.

04:04:24.600 --> 04:04:29.700
Well, 48 states can be divided evenly
into three categories by having 16 each.

04:04:30.100 --> 04:04:32.700
And what the Chi Square does is
it looks at the deviation

04:04:32.700 --> 04:04:37.800
between 24 and 16, ten, 16 and 14 is 16
does some on those.

04:04:37.800 --> 04:04:40.433
And that's
where the value of chi squared comes from.

04:04:40.433 --> 04:04:44.066
And so if we are looking for exactly
the same number of states

04:04:44.066 --> 04:04:47.966
in each category, we have a result
that is significantly different from that.

04:04:48.400 --> 04:04:51.666
But let me close this
and do another analysis

04:04:51.666 --> 04:04:55.133
where we're not looking at
strict equality across the three.

04:04:55.700 --> 04:04:58.533
There are lots of times where you expect

04:04:58.533 --> 04:05:01.466
a different number of people
to be in each category.

04:05:01.766 --> 04:05:03.966
So, for example,
if you're looking at the number of left

04:05:03.966 --> 04:05:06.966
handed and right handed people,
there's more right handed people overall.

04:05:06.966 --> 04:05:10.133
You don't expect it to be split
totally evenly.

04:05:10.466 --> 04:05:15.000
So there is another way you can specify
values other than strict equality.

04:05:15.433 --> 04:05:19.566
Let me put psych regions back in here
and we'll still get expected counts.

04:05:19.966 --> 04:05:23.400
But now I'm going to click on this menu
to get expected proportions.

04:05:23.833 --> 04:05:27.866
And what I can do here,
as you see, is saying that by default it's

04:05:27.900 --> 04:05:29.566
going to split it up across the three.

04:05:29.566 --> 04:05:33.633
So 33.3% change,
I can put it as a different value

04:05:34.233 --> 04:05:37.800
and you put in the ratios here
and that means like 2 to 1

04:05:37.800 --> 04:05:40.866
or 3 to 1 if you want,
you can enter them as percentages.

04:05:40.866 --> 04:05:44.333
So you can say like,
Oh, let's have 60 here

04:05:44.333 --> 04:05:48.366
and let's do 25 here and let's do

04:05:48.700 --> 04:05:51.566
15% here.

04:05:51.966 --> 04:05:57.166
And that gives us the values that we would
expect in each of these conditions.

04:05:58.700 --> 04:06:01.033
I think that adds up to 100.

04:06:01.033 --> 04:06:03.933
And now what you see is our values.

04:06:03.933 --> 04:06:06.033
We have different expect ID values

04:06:07.500 --> 04:06:10.500
and now we still have
a statistically significant result.

04:06:10.800 --> 04:06:14.433
But I was able to specify different values
and you can see how up here

04:06:14.433 --> 04:06:19.033
the expected are 16
and it's the same in each case here.

04:06:19.033 --> 04:06:19.900
The expected values

04:06:19.900 --> 04:06:23.666
change from one to the other
according to the values that I gave it.

04:06:23.666 --> 04:06:26.166
It's still a statistically
significant result.

04:06:26.166 --> 04:06:30.733
In fact, it's, uh, there's a slightly
greater deviation than we had before.

04:06:31.200 --> 04:06:33.133
But the idea here is we have one factor,

04:06:33.133 --> 04:06:36.833
and that is which personality category is
the state put into?

04:06:37.033 --> 04:06:39.700
We have three options
the friendly and conventional,

04:06:39.700 --> 04:06:42.100
the relaxed and creative,
or the temperamental and uninhibited.

04:06:42.433 --> 04:06:45.200
And depending on
how we want to set up our know values,

04:06:45.200 --> 04:06:49.766
we can compare our observed frequencies,
how many there are in each category

04:06:49.966 --> 04:06:51.266
to what we might expect.

04:06:51.266 --> 04:06:55.033
If the null hypothesis of random variation
were true.

04:06:55.266 --> 04:06:56.266
And in both cases here

04:06:56.266 --> 04:07:00.133
we find that we have a statistically
significant deviation from that

04:07:00.366 --> 04:07:04.166
based on using the proportion test
or the chi squared goodness of fit tests.

04:07:04.166 --> 04:07:09.533
In Germany, if you want to look
at the association between two categorical

04:07:09.533 --> 04:07:14.500
or nominal variables, the most common
choice is the chi squared test of

04:07:15.800 --> 04:07:18.600
also called
the chi square test of independence.

04:07:19.233 --> 04:07:24.366
And let me demonstrate how this works
by starting with a split frequency.

04:07:24.366 --> 04:07:27.300
Let's come up here to exploration

04:07:27.300 --> 04:07:29.566
and descriptors and let's look at,

04:07:30.166 --> 04:07:33.033
for instance, psych regions.

04:07:33.033 --> 04:07:36.800
This is in the state data data
set that I supplied.

04:07:37.533 --> 04:07:39.233
I'm going
to put that here under variables,

04:07:39.233 --> 04:07:43.633
but let's split it by whether a state has
a Republican or Democrat governor,

04:07:43.633 --> 04:07:44.766
because it's possible

04:07:44.766 --> 04:07:49.233
that there is some association between
the political party of the governor

04:07:49.233 --> 04:07:53.966
and the state's personality
is rated by some researchers.

04:07:53.966 --> 04:07:57.933
Now, right now,
all it's telling us is that there are

04:07:59.100 --> 04:08:01.266
48 cases in the data.

04:08:01.266 --> 04:08:03.900
I'm going to remove this
extreme permission that I don't

04:08:03.900 --> 04:08:07.766
need.

04:08:07.766 --> 04:08:10.500
Eric, right now,
the table is a little bit smaller.

04:08:10.500 --> 04:08:12.633
I do want to get a frequency table.

04:08:12.700 --> 04:08:14.466
I'll click on that.

04:08:14.466 --> 04:08:17.566
And this is going to tell me
how many people are really

04:08:17.566 --> 04:08:20.200
how many states
there are in each combination.

04:08:20.866 --> 04:08:23.600
And then I'll also ask
for a bar plot at the same time.

04:08:24.600 --> 04:08:25.433
And what we have

04:08:25.433 --> 04:08:28.500
here that we have 20 Republican

04:08:28.500 --> 04:08:31.500
governors of states that are considered
friendly and conventional.

04:08:31.500 --> 04:08:35.166
We have six Democrat governors of states
that are considered temperamental

04:08:35.166 --> 04:08:38.366
and uninhibited and we can see that
in the chart down here.

04:08:38.366 --> 04:08:40.200
Again,
I apologize for the overlapping labels.

04:08:40.200 --> 04:08:42.633
I'm sure that'll be fixed in the later
version.

04:08:43.300 --> 04:08:46.166
The blue lines are Democrat

04:08:46.233 --> 04:08:49.266
and the yellow gold ones are Republicans.

04:08:49.266 --> 04:08:52.800
And what you see is this huge spike
in terms of friendly and conventional.

04:08:52.800 --> 04:08:56.233
The vast majority of those states
have Republican governors

04:08:56.233 --> 04:09:00.100
where the other ones appear to be
somewhat split, a little more Republicans,

04:09:00.400 --> 04:09:02.133
the temperamental and uninhibited.

04:09:02.133 --> 04:09:05.100
But let's find out whether this difference
is statistically significant,

04:09:05.100 --> 04:09:08.000
whether there is
in a statistical association

04:09:08.566 --> 04:09:12.000
between the personality of the state

04:09:12.366 --> 04:09:15.700
and whether the governor is Democrat
or Republican.

04:09:15.700 --> 04:09:19.333
So we'll use the chi squared
test of association for that.

04:09:19.333 --> 04:09:22.100
Let's come up here and go to frequencies

04:09:22.100 --> 04:09:25.400
and then I come down here
to independent samples.

04:09:25.400 --> 04:09:27.700
What that means is
we have different groups of people

04:09:27.966 --> 04:09:30.966
in each of these categories
or different groups of states

04:09:31.500 --> 04:09:36.166
and it's chi squared test of association
also called the test for independence.

04:09:36.166 --> 04:09:39.066
And I'll click on that
and here's what it's going to ask me.

04:09:39.300 --> 04:09:44.266
I need to give it the rows and the columns
for what's called a contingency table.

04:09:44.266 --> 04:09:46.500
That's just a table of rows and columns.

04:09:46.833 --> 04:09:50.766
So I'm going to start by putting psych
region as the rows

04:09:51.233 --> 04:09:54.700
and I'm going to put governor
as the columns that will mirror the table

04:09:54.900 --> 04:09:58.766
I already created
and I could add extra layers,

04:09:58.766 --> 04:10:01.800
but that gets really complicated
and hard to interpret.

04:10:02.200 --> 04:10:04.766
What that would
mean, by the way, is putting in another

04:10:05.366 --> 04:10:06.866
categorical variable here.

04:10:06.866 --> 04:10:09.533
So we'd end up with a
three dimensional table, but

04:10:10.866 --> 04:10:12.933
you don't
want to deal with that's too hard.

04:10:12.933 --> 04:10:14.700
We have a choice for statistics chi.

04:10:14.700 --> 04:10:16.866
Squared is just fine for what I have here.

04:10:16.866 --> 04:10:19.766
These ones, the comparative measures,
including the confidence intervals,

04:10:20.100 --> 04:10:22.600
only apply when you have
what's called a two by two table.

04:10:22.600 --> 04:10:24.933
And that means two rows and two columns.

04:10:24.933 --> 04:10:27.000
We have three rows and two columns.

04:10:27.000 --> 04:10:29.066
So these ones don't apply
to what we're doing.

04:10:29.700 --> 04:10:31.266
We have some other choices.

04:10:31.266 --> 04:10:35.066
We can get a contingency coefficient
or a Phi and Cramer's V,

04:10:35.833 --> 04:10:40.133
let's hit a contingency coefficient
and that's like a correlation coefficient.

04:10:40.133 --> 04:10:42.900
Here at the bottom
we can do some ordinal statistics.

04:10:42.900 --> 04:10:44.666
We have categorical data, not ordinal.

04:10:44.666 --> 04:10:46.400
So I'm going to leave that alone.

04:10:46.400 --> 04:10:49.233
And then we have some choices about what
we put in the table.

04:10:49.233 --> 04:10:53.400
Now, right now what we have
are just observed frequencies.

04:10:53.400 --> 04:10:55.900
The number of states
that fall into each combination.

04:10:56.533 --> 04:10:58.733
We can also put expected frequencies.

04:10:58.733 --> 04:11:00.166
And that's important because that's

04:11:00.166 --> 04:11:03.566
what the chi squared test
is, comparing these observed values too.

04:11:03.933 --> 04:11:07.766
So For instance, up here in friendly
conventional, we have four states

04:11:07.766 --> 04:11:11.100
with Democratic governors,
but if the data were

04:11:11.100 --> 04:11:14.833
completely randomly distributed,
it would be seven and a half.

04:11:15.466 --> 04:11:17.900
On the other hand, down here
we have eight states

04:11:17.933 --> 04:11:20.633
that are temperamental and uninhibited
and have a Republican governor.

04:11:21.366 --> 04:11:24.433
But we would expect,
if things were random, to have 9.3.

04:11:24.600 --> 04:11:28.766
The way it gets that, by the way,
is by multiplying the column totals

04:11:29.100 --> 04:11:34.533
by the raw totals and then dividing
by the grand total for each of the cells.

04:11:34.533 --> 04:11:36.566
But the important part stays the same.

04:11:36.633 --> 04:11:40.133
Down here we have our chi squared test
and we get a value of chi

04:11:40.133 --> 04:11:42.866
squared of 4.89
with two degrees of freedom.

04:11:43.200 --> 04:11:46.200
We a probability value of 0.087.

04:11:46.533 --> 04:11:50.333
Now that's something that's
not very likely to happen by chance,

04:11:50.333 --> 04:11:54.133
but because it's greater
than the standard cutoff of 0.05,

04:11:54.400 --> 04:11:58.200
we can clear that even with this
really big difference right here,

04:11:58.566 --> 04:12:02.066
that these results
are not statistically different from what

04:12:02.066 --> 04:12:05.100
we would expect through random variation.

04:12:05.366 --> 04:12:08.200
So even though it looks like
there's a big difference, it doesn't

04:12:08.200 --> 04:12:11.166
hold up under null hypothesis testing.

04:12:11.466 --> 04:12:14.200
And that's one of the values
of doing the Chi Square test,

04:12:14.466 --> 04:12:17.900
even something that looks at
an eyeball level like a big result.

04:12:17.966 --> 04:12:20.666
When you do the actual numbers
and get the inferential test,

04:12:21.000 --> 04:12:24.566
it may tell you a different story, which
is exactly happened in this situation.

04:12:25.700 --> 04:12:26.500
One of the things

04:12:26.500 --> 04:12:31.133
I really love about your movie
is that they include some really unusual

04:12:31.133 --> 04:12:34.433
or specialty tests,
make them really easy to use.

04:12:34.700 --> 04:12:38.300
One of these is McNamara's test,
and this is a test designed

04:12:38.300 --> 04:12:39.400
for contingency tables.

04:12:39.400 --> 04:12:43.300
But instead of being independent
variables, it's for repeated.

04:12:43.300 --> 04:12:44.466
Now, I have to admit,

04:12:44.466 --> 04:12:47.733
I've been doing research for 30 years
and I've never had to do this.

04:12:48.066 --> 04:12:51.066
But it's always good to know
that when you come up against

04:12:51.066 --> 04:12:54.100
this kind of question,
you can get the right tool,

04:12:54.100 --> 04:12:56.766
which gives you greater power
and precision in your research.

04:12:57.100 --> 04:13:01.566
So McNamara's test is four categories,
and it is for frequencies

04:13:01.566 --> 04:13:04.433
in those categories,
but where the categories are repeated.

04:13:04.600 --> 04:13:08.266
Now what I'm doing here is a little bit
different in my other examples.

04:13:08.266 --> 04:13:13.033
I've had one row per observation
and so I've had these long datasets.

04:13:13.533 --> 04:13:17.000
Right now I'm using a summary table
and it's kind of nice

04:13:17.000 --> 04:13:19.366
that Jamovi lets
you do this in a number of situations.

04:13:20.266 --> 04:13:23.833
That way I can do it this instead
of having like 85 rows of data.

04:13:24.433 --> 04:13:27.900
The data that I'm using actually comes
from a table that I saw

04:13:28.166 --> 04:13:30.733
on the Wikipedia article
for McNamara's test,

04:13:31.033 --> 04:13:33.900
and it's about patients
with Hodgkin's disease

04:13:34.233 --> 04:13:37.400
and whether they and their siblings
had tonsillectomy.

04:13:37.433 --> 04:13:39.566
I don't know the association
between the two,

04:13:39.566 --> 04:13:42.900
but it's an interesting example
because the sibling goes

04:13:42.900 --> 04:13:46.066
with the patient, so there's a connection
between the two of them.

04:13:46.500 --> 04:13:48.266
Let me show you how we set this up.

04:13:48.266 --> 04:13:52.833
Using McNamara's test in Germany,
we come to frequencies

04:13:52.833 --> 04:13:56.666
and we come down right here
and they call it McNamara test.

04:13:56.666 --> 04:14:01.700
If you go to Wikipedia, McNamara's
possessive test, I'm going click on that.

04:14:03.133 --> 04:14:05.633
And what
we need to do is simply take our data

04:14:05.633 --> 04:14:09.933
put in what variable has the row labels,
what variable has the column labels?

04:14:09.933 --> 04:14:12.800
And then the variable
that has the counter,

04:14:12.800 --> 04:14:14.100
the actual frequencies themselves.

04:14:14.100 --> 04:14:17.100
That's because I'm setting this up
as a summary table,

04:14:17.400 --> 04:14:20.066
so I'm going to take patient
and put that in rows

04:14:21.300 --> 04:14:23.600
and you can see how it starts
to fill it in right here.

04:14:24.100 --> 04:14:27.766
And then we take sibling and put it
into columns and it fills in the table,

04:14:27.766 --> 04:14:30.766
but it doesn't have any values yet
except to say that.

04:14:30.766 --> 04:14:32.700
In theory, there's just one of each.

04:14:32.700 --> 04:14:36.600
But then I take the N that's a variable
that tells you how many cases

04:14:36.600 --> 04:14:37.633
there are in each category.

04:14:37.633 --> 04:14:40.633
And I put that right here
and it fills in the table

04:14:40.633 --> 04:14:42.200
and it does a couple of things.

04:14:42.200 --> 04:14:46.266
Number one,
it gives us that there are 37 cases where

04:14:46.500 --> 04:14:50.500
the patient with Hodgkin's did not have
a tonsillectomy and did the sibling.

04:14:50.966 --> 04:14:53.700
But there are 26 where they both

04:14:53.700 --> 04:14:57.000
had tonsillectomy,
and so there's an association in that way.

04:14:57.633 --> 04:15:00.300
We also have the actual result
here, McNamara says, is

04:15:00.300 --> 04:15:02.600
based on the value of chi squared.

04:15:02.600 --> 04:15:06.233
And it's interpreted in the same way
that if the value of P,

04:15:06.233 --> 04:15:10.566
the probability value
or the probability of the observed effect

04:15:10.566 --> 04:15:15.333
size,
if the null hypothesis is true, is .088.

04:15:15.333 --> 04:15:19.300
Now that doesn't meet the standard levels
of statistical significance.

04:15:19.300 --> 04:15:20.800
It's in the direction.

04:15:20.800 --> 04:15:21.900
I do want to show you

04:15:21.900 --> 04:15:24.500
a couple of other things we can do
that can be really helpful,

04:15:24.900 --> 04:15:26.800
and that is anytime
you're dealing with a table

04:15:26.800 --> 04:15:29.700
and especially when you have samples,
different sizes.

04:15:30.033 --> 04:15:34.100
Now you see we have 44 Hodgkins patients
that did not have tonsillectomy

04:15:34.133 --> 04:15:35.166
and 41 that did.

04:15:35.166 --> 04:15:38.500
Those numbers are really close
to each other, so they're easy to compare.

04:15:38.500 --> 04:15:42.533
But for the siblings is 52 and 33
and that makes the comparison

04:15:42.533 --> 04:15:43.800
a little more difficult.

04:15:43.800 --> 04:15:47.366
And so what you can do
are get row and column percentages.

04:15:47.800 --> 04:15:50.533
Now do just row percentages first.

04:15:51.200 --> 04:15:55.433
What this means is that for the patients
that did not have a tonsillectomy,

04:15:55.466 --> 04:16:00.600
84% of their siblings also did
not a tonsillectomy, whereas 16% did.

04:16:01.033 --> 04:16:04.100
So they add up to 100% going across.

04:16:04.100 --> 04:16:05.700
And that's going to be true
all the way across.

04:16:05.700 --> 04:16:09.433
So this really just tells us
that 60.2% of the siblings

04:16:10.133 --> 04:16:13.600
did not have tonsillectomy and 38.8% did.

04:16:14.966 --> 04:16:18.266
Independent of that, you can
get column percentages going to turn off

04:16:18.300 --> 04:16:21.900
the row percentages for just a moment
and we'll get column percent.

04:16:22.266 --> 04:16:26.000
And now what this does is it
adds up to 100% going down.

04:16:26.300 --> 04:16:30.833
And this is what's nice when you have
different overall frequencies.

04:16:31.166 --> 04:16:35.033
So we have just 52 people
without tonsillectomy as 33 that did.

04:16:35.600 --> 04:16:40.433
And so we can say that of the
that did not have a tonsillectomy, 71.2%

04:16:40.833 --> 04:16:47.066
had patients, siblings who did not
and 28.8 had patient siblings who did.

04:16:47.500 --> 04:16:50.700
And we can compare
that to the percentages over here.

04:16:50.900 --> 04:16:51.900
And that allows us to make it

04:16:51.900 --> 04:16:55.600
sort of an apples to apples comparison,
even though the total frequencies

04:16:55.600 --> 04:16:58.266
are different because often
you're more concerned about the rates.

04:16:58.500 --> 04:17:01.200
And that's something you can do with
any kind of chi squared table.

04:17:01.200 --> 04:17:05.200
You can do it in the binomial,
you can do it in the chi squared test

04:17:05.200 --> 04:17:08.000
for goodness of fit
for the chi square test of associations

04:17:08.500 --> 04:17:11.433
that getting the percentages
is often easier to interpret.

04:17:11.700 --> 04:17:15.300
And the same thing is true
in McNamara's test, which allows you to do

04:17:15.300 --> 04:17:17.700
continued in table four paired data.

04:17:19.200 --> 04:17:19.700
Another

04:17:19.700 --> 04:17:23.666
surprising but useful option in Jamovi
for analyzing frequency

04:17:23.666 --> 04:17:27.900
data and associations
between them is log linear regression.

04:17:28.200 --> 04:17:31.800
Now what this is is a form of regression
where instead of trying

04:17:31.800 --> 04:17:36.200
to predict a person's score
on a quantitative or continuous

04:17:36.200 --> 04:17:40.600
outcome variable, you're trying to predict
the number of observations,

04:17:40.600 --> 04:17:44.033
the frequencies
within cells of a contingency table.

04:17:44.666 --> 04:17:47.200
It's actually now it's a powerful method,

04:17:47.200 --> 04:17:50.066
although it can be confusing
to read the output.

04:17:50.400 --> 04:17:54.566
My goal here is simply to show you
that it's easy to put these together

04:17:54.766 --> 04:17:57.233
into a movie. You decide
that this is something you need.

04:17:57.466 --> 04:18:00.666
Then you're going to want to consult
your other resources on how to design

04:18:00.666 --> 04:18:05.566
and how to interpret a log linear
regression, but calculating it into movie.

04:18:05.566 --> 04:18:06.833
Very simple.

04:18:06.833 --> 04:18:07.566
Let's do this.

