04:23:04.533 --> 04:23:07.200
Our next topic, engine movie is factors.

04:23:07.200 --> 04:23:08.700
And the idea here is that really

04:23:08.700 --> 04:23:12.800
data is a jungle and it can be confusing
and it can overwhelming.

04:23:12.800 --> 04:23:17.166
And truthfully, sometimes
less is more specifically with data.

04:23:17.166 --> 04:23:20.000
What that means is going
from a thousand variables

04:23:20.200 --> 04:23:22.733
to maybe a dozen or two
that you need to deal with.

04:23:22.733 --> 04:23:25.433
It's much easier to find the order
and meaning in that

04:23:25.666 --> 04:23:28.466
you also tend to have more reliable
and stable measurements.

04:23:29.033 --> 04:23:33.300
And the first procedure we'll look at into
movie is analysis because, you know,

04:23:33.533 --> 04:23:34.366
sometimes you want to

04:23:34.366 --> 04:23:37.800
talk about the flock, the whole,
and not each individual sheep.

04:23:38.133 --> 04:23:41.800
Perhaps you want to combine the variables
you need to get a single scale score.

04:23:42.666 --> 04:23:45.600
But to do that, you first
need to make sure that you're dealing

04:23:45.600 --> 04:23:48.500
with similar variables,
that they're measuring similar things

04:23:48.900 --> 04:23:52.700
and reliability analysis, whether
with chromebox Alpha or McDonald's.

04:23:52.700 --> 04:23:54.733
Iomega can allow you to do that,

04:23:55.700 --> 04:23:58.466
or if
you're trying to find the main dimensions

04:23:58.466 --> 04:24:00.666
that make up your data,
like finding the main streets

04:24:00.666 --> 04:24:04.000
in the city,
you can try a principal component analysis

04:24:04.466 --> 04:24:07.500
or the closely related exploratory factor
analysis,

04:24:07.800 --> 04:24:11.000
which well being based on different
mathematics and a different

04:24:11.600 --> 04:24:14.966
about the relationship
between observed variables and implicit

04:24:14.966 --> 04:24:17.700
factors accomplishes
basically the same thing.

04:24:18.000 --> 04:24:19.466
And both of these approaches

04:24:19.466 --> 04:24:23.066
are exceptionally easy
set up and interpret into movie.

04:24:23.633 --> 04:24:28.133
Or maybe you already know the beans
that you want the variables to go into,

04:24:28.366 --> 04:24:33.933
and in that case you can use confirmatory
factor analysis where you put together

04:24:34.233 --> 04:24:38.066
the factors that the variables go into
and the observed variables

04:24:38.066 --> 04:24:41.366
and you see how well the variables
match up with those factors

04:24:41.666 --> 04:24:44.500
and how well your model
explains the covariance in your data.

04:24:45.500 --> 04:24:48.700
But whichever technique you use,
it'll help you get some clarity

04:24:48.700 --> 04:24:53.033
in your data so you can start on path
towards insight and action.

04:24:54.333 --> 04:24:57.333
In design, you'll hear
the saying less is more,

04:24:57.333 --> 04:25:00.900
and the same thing is true
to a certain extent in data analysis.

04:25:00.900 --> 04:25:03.900
Instead of trying to analyze
50 different variables,

04:25:04.133 --> 04:25:08.900
why don't you collapse them into a smaller
number of more manageable variables?

04:25:09.100 --> 04:25:13.800
That's going to give you more reliable
information and more stable insights.

04:25:14.133 --> 04:25:18.533
But in order to do this, you need to first
establish that you can collapse

04:25:18.533 --> 04:25:23.400
the data, that there are correlations
you're trying to combine, like with, like.

04:25:23.933 --> 04:25:27.666
And the most common way of doing
this is with a reliability analysis

04:25:27.700 --> 04:25:28.966
which looks at

04:25:28.966 --> 04:25:32.333
the relationship of several variables
that you would like to combine.

04:25:32.933 --> 04:25:35.533
This is very common
if you're working with survey data

