04:39:52.866 --> 04:39:56.133
Now, this is probably
one of the most important things

04:39:56.133 --> 04:40:00.133
you need to do when you're analyzing
survey data or, a number of variables

04:40:00.133 --> 04:40:02.500
that might potentially be correlated
with each other.

04:40:02.800 --> 04:40:05.633
A principal component analysis
is going to allow you to determine

04:40:05.900 --> 04:40:08.500
the underlying structure
and see what you can

04:40:09.100 --> 04:40:12.400
with each other to simplify the data
that you have to deal with

04:40:12.400 --> 04:40:16.133
and hopefully have more reliable
information at the same time,

04:40:17.666 --> 04:40:19.866
sometimes you're analyzing data.

04:40:19.866 --> 04:40:22.966
You have to draw distinctions
that don't make much of a difference.

04:40:23.233 --> 04:40:26.800
And one of those distinctions
is between principal component

04:40:26.800 --> 04:40:29.366
analysis and exploratory factor analysis.

04:40:29.833 --> 04:40:34.133
Now, these are based on profoundly
different mathematics,

04:40:34.500 --> 04:40:38.200
and they also have a different philosophy
about the relationship

04:40:38.200 --> 04:40:42.133
between the individual items
that you got data from, from people

04:40:42.466 --> 04:40:47.966
and the implied
or implicit factor behind them.

04:40:47.966 --> 04:40:51.733
But the fact is they look really,

04:40:51.733 --> 04:40:55.800
really similar
and people use them to do the same things.

04:40:56.233 --> 04:40:58.633
And there's so much commonality.

04:40:58.633 --> 04:41:00.766
There is sometimes hard to remember
which one you're doing.

04:41:01.733 --> 04:41:04.266
I've showed you
how to do principal component analysis.

04:41:04.266 --> 04:41:08.700
I'm now going to show you how to do
