spreadsheets and if you've learned

04:53:26.700 --> 04:53:30.900
how to use your movie, then tool wise,
maybe you're there now.

04:53:30.900 --> 04:53:33.133
Maybe you have what you need.

04:53:33.133 --> 04:53:36.533
I'm firmly of the belief
that spreadsheets into movie cover

04:53:36.533 --> 04:53:42.066
the analytic necessities for the vast
majority of people who work with data.

04:53:42.066 --> 04:53:45.633
Now it's possible that you might need
some more tools

04:53:45.633 --> 04:53:47.700
depending on the kind of work you do.

04:53:47.700 --> 04:53:48.833
But not everybody needs it.

04:53:49.966 --> 04:53:53.166
If you're going to do something
that is advanced

04:53:53.166 --> 04:53:56.466
than what Jamovi does and Jamovi
has a lot of capability.

04:53:57.433 --> 04:54:01.100
Then you might need to do some programing
for data analysis,

04:54:01.100 --> 04:54:05.300
and that usually means either
are the statistical programing language, R

04:54:05.900 --> 04:54:10.333
or Python with the collection of data
packages that are available for it.

04:54:10.733 --> 04:54:13.800
Those two are very,
very popular in data science

04:54:14.200 --> 04:54:18.800
and I like to use R a lot,
but I much prefer working with Jamovi.

04:54:18.800 --> 04:54:24.166
For the vast majority of my analyzes,
there are other helpful tools.

04:54:24.166 --> 04:54:26.266
If you're looking for something
to add to your toolkit.

04:54:26.600 --> 04:54:29.866
First, there's sequel structured
query language

04:54:29.866 --> 04:54:32.600
for data access,
data manipulation, data cleaning.

04:54:32.933 --> 04:54:34.900
If you're working in

04:54:34.900 --> 04:54:38.633
an organizational setting,
then sequel can be really, really helpful.

04:54:38.966 --> 04:54:41.700
And truthfully,
you only have to learn about 20 commands

04:54:41.700 --> 04:54:44.366
to get most of what you need
out of sequel.

04:54:44.366 --> 04:54:49.200
Second is Tableau is a proprietary
desktop application.

04:54:49.200 --> 04:54:52.000
Now there's Tableau Public, which is free.

04:54:52.000 --> 04:54:54.800
All the work you do is available
free on the Internet for everybody.

04:54:55.100 --> 04:54:58.500
And there's Tableau desktop,
which if you're a regular person

04:54:58.500 --> 04:55:01.633
or if your company
is extraordinarily expensive

04:55:01.633 --> 04:55:03.700
if you work for a nonprofit,
you can get it for free.

04:55:04.000 --> 04:55:09.266
Tableau really is the tool of choice
for interactive visualization, even for

04:55:09.466 --> 04:55:14.400
data scientists who are handy with code
and then third presentation software.

04:55:14.400 --> 04:55:15.200
Because remember,

04:55:15.200 --> 04:55:17.900
you do all this analysis,
you're going to have to communicate it

04:55:17.900 --> 04:55:21.200
to somebody, you have to share it
with them and knowing how to use

04:55:21.566 --> 04:55:26.066
Microsoft, PowerPoint or Apple Keynote
or Google Slides

04:55:26.600 --> 04:55:30.866
is going to go a very long way in terms
of making your work professionally,

04:55:30.866 --> 04:55:34.800
meaning For now,
you may have seen this chart before.

04:55:34.800 --> 04:55:36.933
If you're coming from the world of data
science, you've seen

04:55:36.933 --> 04:55:38.166
this is called the data science

04:55:38.166 --> 04:55:41.566
Venn diagram, and it was created by Drew
Conway several years ago.

04:55:42.133 --> 04:55:44.933
And he said that data science
consists of three different

04:55:44.933 --> 04:55:48.066
fields put together at the top
left is coding.

04:55:48.066 --> 04:55:52.166
That's working with computers at the top
right is stats

04:55:52.200 --> 04:55:56.100
and statistics in mathematics,
and at the bottom is domain knowledge.

04:55:56.100 --> 04:55:58.433
You actually have to understand
the field that you're working in.

04:55:58.766 --> 04:56:03.100
And when you take the three of those
and combine, then you get data science

04:56:03.900 --> 04:56:07.633
and it applies to a certain extent
with what we're doing.

04:56:07.633 --> 04:56:09.300
Even in this primary situation.

04:56:09.300 --> 04:56:11.066
We've been talking about the tool

04:56:11.066 --> 04:56:13.600
that's going to be that red circle
on the top left.

04:56:14.066 --> 04:56:17.100
We have not talked
very much about the statistics.

04:56:17.100 --> 04:56:22.200
That's the conceptual element,
nor the domain, the application.

04:56:22.700 --> 04:56:26.266
And so even if you have
the greatest toolset in the world,

04:56:26.933 --> 04:56:30.633
there are these other areas
that do need your attention.

04:56:30.633 --> 04:56:35.333
And so I would say probably the single
most important thing is data fluency.

04:56:35.666 --> 04:56:40.066
And seriously reading a bar chart
and a line chart is if you know

04:56:40.066 --> 04:56:44.400
how, to ask a meaningful question
and know how to read

04:56:44.400 --> 04:56:48.966
even these very simple analyzes
to get something that is insightful

04:56:48.966 --> 04:56:53.033
and actionable out of it, you're
going to have huge value in what you do.

04:56:53.666 --> 04:56:56.966
I actually believe that bar charts
and line charts and scatter plots

04:56:57.533 --> 04:57:01.466
probably cover
98% of the data visualization needs.

04:57:01.466 --> 04:57:05.066
For most people, the simple tools
are often the most effective.

04:57:05.066 --> 04:57:06.600
So one of the reasons
I really liked your movie,

04:57:06.600 --> 04:57:10.066
even though it allows you
to do some complex things, but data.

04:57:10.066 --> 04:57:14.133
And so here at DataLab,
we're developing courses to teach data,

04:57:14.133 --> 04:57:18.133
fluency, the concepts and the principles
that the tools help you with.

04:57:18.133 --> 04:57:20.600
But the tools are not a substitute them.

04:57:20.600 --> 04:57:23.233
And of course separate from the principles
and the tools

04:57:23.666 --> 04:57:26.333
is the ability
to work in a particular setting.

04:57:27.733 --> 04:57:28.966
So much of the real work

04:57:28.966 --> 04:57:32.466
in any data project is not the actual
working with the numbers, but

04:57:32.466 --> 04:57:35.500
is in talking with people to understand
what you're trying to accomplish

04:57:35.933 --> 04:57:39.000
and seeing how you can make sense of it
and what to do with it.

04:57:39.300 --> 04:57:42.300
And the best way to do
this is to work together with other people

04:57:42.300 --> 04:57:45.333
on real data projects
to answer real questions.

04:57:45.733 --> 04:57:47.700
You can do that at your current job.

04:57:47.700 --> 04:57:52.233
You can do it with a volunteer group,
you can do some online freelancing.

04:57:52.233 --> 04:57:55.200
Any of these are great ways
to get the experience you need.

04:57:56.233 --> 04:57:59.733
Movie is an excellent data tool,
but You need to have

04:57:59.733 --> 04:58:03.333
some understanding of these statistical
concepts, the data fluency

04:58:03.900 --> 04:58:06.866
and more than anything,
the ability to apply it

04:58:07.100 --> 04:58:09.366
when working with
real people on real settings.

04:58:10.333 --> 04:58:12.833
So we've been talking about a tool

04:58:12.833 --> 04:58:16.266
and you have these other possibilities
that we talk about a data lab as well,

04:58:16.933 --> 04:58:21.833
but take them together, take them together
and really you're good to go.

04:58:21.866 --> 04:58:23.833
You've got what you need.

